1
00:00:00,640 --> 00:00:05,520
They rip, they tear, they scoop, they haul.

2
00:00:05,860 --> 00:00:09,920
They are among the biggest and strongest machines ever built.

3
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:15,080
They are heroes in the most astounding construction projects in the world

4
00:00:15,081 --> 00:00:19,160
and occasional culprits costing environmental destruction.

5
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:27,240
Now, experience the power to move mountains with Construction Machines on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,000
In Southern California, earth-moving machines are creating a new dam.

7
00:00:37,001 --> 00:00:50,000
In terms of cubic yards of earth moved, this project is 14 times the size of the famous Hoover Dam, yet it barely makes headlines.

8
00:00:50,001 --> 00:00:53,011
We're located in Winchester, California.

9
00:00:53,012 --> 00:00:57,000
It's about halfway between San Diego and Los Angeles.

10
00:00:57,001 --> 00:01:02,400
and Los Angeles and we're under construction of the largest earth-filled

11
00:01:02,401 --> 00:01:05,500
dam in North America.

12
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:13,940
The entire Eastside Reservoir project including the East Dam and the West Dam

13
00:01:13,941 --> 00:01:18,500
which we have is approximately 150 million cubic yards.

14
00:01:18,501 --> 00:01:23,500
The pipeline that comes from the Colorado River 248 miles to the east

15
00:01:23,501 --> 00:01:28,500
crosses the earthquake rift zone of the San Andreas Fault.

16
00:01:28,501 --> 00:01:35,500
Water District engineers figure that one day the big one will strike and they would need to have a reserve.

17
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,500
The amazing speed with which this new reservoir is being built would not be possible without today's state-of-the-art earth-moving equipment.

18
00:01:45,501 --> 00:01:55,020
Today's large mining trucks like the 785CAT are so sophisticated that they tell and record

19
00:01:55,021 --> 00:02:02,740
everything for the operator and for the mechanic and for management.

20
00:02:02,741 --> 00:02:05,820
We have water trucks out here that just absolutely amaze me.

21
00:02:05,821 --> 00:02:08,300
We haul 20,000 gallons per load.

22
00:02:08,301 --> 00:02:13,400
And what really amazes you is the way that the computer programs and so much of the systems

23
00:02:13,401 --> 00:02:18,360
are controlled by electronic modules and computerization

24
00:02:18,361 --> 00:02:24,060
where we can download information to tell basically what that machine,

25
00:02:24,180 --> 00:02:26,620
the function of that machine for an entire 24-hour period.

26
00:02:27,300 --> 00:02:28,780
We never had that before.

27
00:02:29,240 --> 00:02:31,420
We thought we knew it all, but we didn't.

28
00:02:34,940 --> 00:02:40,420
Huge Ingersoll-ran drills create holes 45 feet deep in the solid rock.

29
00:02:40,421 --> 00:02:42,060
One iron hole.

30
00:02:43,400 --> 00:02:57,400
After explosive charges are detonated, a caterpillar shovel weighing 350 tons moves in and scoops up the earth and rock into enormous trucks which drive it away.

31
00:03:05,400 --> 00:03:08,900
For every pass, he's putting 23 cubic yards into that truck.

32
00:03:08,901 --> 00:03:14,180
and each cubic yard of rock weighs about 4,000 pounds.

33
00:03:16,260 --> 00:03:19,620
He can load the truck in a minute and 15 seconds,

34
00:03:20,760 --> 00:03:22,940
four to five passes,

35
00:03:23,100 --> 00:03:25,140
depending upon the density of the material that he's loaded.

36
00:03:27,320 --> 00:03:29,840
All this equipment has enabled the project

37
00:03:29,841 --> 00:03:32,220
to move forward with fewer people on the payroll,

38
00:03:32,221 --> 00:03:34,560
since the machines can work far faster

39
00:03:34,561 --> 00:03:36,380
than those of a generation ago.

40
00:03:36,381 --> 00:03:39,380
But the price tag for this is high.

41
00:03:39,381 --> 00:03:42,380
The trucks were $1.1 million.

42
00:03:42,381 --> 00:03:46,380
5230 cost $3.2 million.

43
00:03:46,381 --> 00:03:50,380
The reservoir will hold 269 billion gallons of water.

44
00:03:50,381 --> 00:03:54,380
It's going to be 2 miles wide and 4.5 miles long.

45
00:03:54,381 --> 00:03:58,380
It sounded big when we came, but day by day we're whittling at it,

46
00:03:58,381 --> 00:04:01,380
and it just happens.

47
00:04:01,381 --> 00:04:03,380
Not a problem.

48
00:04:06,380 --> 00:04:09,653
The business of moving the earth is nothing new.

49
00:04:09,654 --> 00:04:12,380
If we look back far enough, human beings

50
00:04:12,381 --> 00:04:18,882
found it necessary to dig dirt with the first earthmovers, their bare hands.

51
00:04:18,883 --> 00:04:20,080
Eons ago, some

52
00:04:20,081 --> 00:04:26,465
unknown inventors fashioned the first tools, a shovel, a pick, a hole.

53
00:04:26,466 --> 00:04:28,380
This ancient Egyptian

54
00:04:28,381 --> 00:04:32,380
wielding a hole is breaking ground for a canal.

55
00:04:32,381 --> 00:04:43,400
We got the major projects in the ancient times, such as the giant pyramids of Egypt, the Great War of China, and a lot of other projects.

56
00:04:43,401 --> 00:04:51,160
They were all built by thousands and thousands of laborers, toiling away without any mechanized means whatsoever.

57
00:04:51,161 --> 00:05:03,419
One of the first steps forward in the development of earth moving occurred when some unknown dreamer got the idea of putting boards together between two shafts to form

58
00:05:03,420 --> 00:05:04,960
the first handbarrow.

59
00:05:06,300 --> 00:05:16,000
Not content with this arrangement, some inventor, probably in China, got the idea of attaching a wheel to one end and the first wheelbarrow was born.

60
00:05:16,001 --> 00:05:24,000
In ancient Egypt, over 4,000 years ago, something resembling a sled was used to haul heavy burdens.

61
00:05:24,001 --> 00:05:29,000
Round sticks or logs were placed underneath it to diminish friction.

62
00:05:29,001 --> 00:05:35,000
Now, more dirt or big rocks could be hauled.

63
00:05:35,001 --> 00:05:41,000
Between 200 and 250 BC, the Romans started work on their vast highway system.

64
00:05:41,001 --> 00:05:48,000
2200 years later, the remains of the Appian Way still stretch south from Rome to southern Italy.

65
00:05:48,001 --> 00:05:54,000
Using similar earth-moving methods, the Romans also dug mines for metals,

66
00:05:54,001 --> 00:06:01,000
providing us with an early example of the possible dangers associated with disrupting the earth.

67
00:06:01,001 --> 00:06:09,400
Those were very small mines, and the volume of material that they were moving was being

68
00:06:09,401 --> 00:06:16,500
done by pick and shovel and by burrows and little carts, not by the kinds of vast machines

69
00:06:16,501 --> 00:06:19,280
that we're using today.

70
00:06:19,281 --> 00:06:25,300
And we know that we're getting acid drainage and water contamination still flowing today

71
00:06:25,301 --> 00:06:30,360
from mines that were dug during the Roman Empire in Europe.

72
00:06:30,361 --> 00:06:35,000
The development of earth moving tools was extremely slow.

73
00:06:35,001 --> 00:06:40,240
The same basic practical two-wheeled cart was used virtually unchanged by the ancient

74
00:06:40,241 --> 00:06:46,640
Egyptians, medieval British and French peasants, and early American farmers.

75
00:06:46,641 --> 00:06:54,240
With it, manpower could be augmented by horse power and mule power.

76
00:06:54,241 --> 00:06:58,860
When the first Europeans came across the Atlantic Ocean, they found the Native Americans using

77
00:06:58,861 --> 00:07:03,870
baskets, some of the oldest means devised by man to haul earth.

78
00:07:03,871 --> 00:07:05,540
The large mounds left

79
00:07:05,541 --> 00:07:11,120
by tribes throughout eastern North America, some of them hundreds of feet high, were probably

80
00:07:11,121 --> 00:07:13,534
built with the aid of baskets.

81
00:07:13,535 --> 00:07:18,040
Around the time of the American Revolution, earth-moving

82
00:07:18,041 --> 00:07:19,900
tools began to change.

83
00:07:19,901 --> 00:07:25,480
In a few decades, the Ames family of Massachusetts transformed the

84
00:07:25,481 --> 00:07:29,480
shovel, mankind's basic earth-moving tool for thousands of years.

85
00:07:30,860 --> 00:07:33,600
The patriarch John Ames, an ambitious blacksmith,

86
00:07:33,760 --> 00:07:36,680
began producing shovels in 1776.

87
00:07:37,280 --> 00:07:41,480
His son Oliver relocated their forge on the Tauten River

88
00:07:41,481 --> 00:07:45,340
at Northeastern. Their old colony

89
00:07:45,341 --> 00:07:48,480
shovel was four pounds lighter than its British competitor.

90
00:07:49,740 --> 00:07:53,180
Although it looked like its handmade ancestor, this shovel

91
00:07:53,181 --> 00:07:57,680
was factory-made, featuring steel plate with welded iron straps.

92
00:07:59,620 --> 00:08:05,680
By the 1840s, Ames shovels were forged at 2,000 degrees in sophisticated furnaces.

93
00:08:06,500 --> 00:08:11,860
Soon, Ames was one of the country's first industrial dynasties. The plant turned out

94
00:08:11,861 --> 00:08:17,160
20,000 shovels a year, shovels that went to work digging the Erie Canal across New York

95
00:08:17,161 --> 00:08:19,409
and gold in California's foothills.

96
00:08:19,410 --> 00:08:22,880
The company prospered, and the rest of America charged

97
00:08:22,881 --> 00:08:28,040
forward into a new age of industrialization, which no one could have imagined.

98
00:08:29,860 --> 00:08:36,100
The largest tires currently manufactured for earth-moving dump trucks are made by the Bridgestone

99
00:08:36,101 --> 00:08:41,213
Corporation of Tokyo. They measure 12 and a half feet in diameter.

100
00:08:41,214 --> 00:08:42,840
Construction machines

101
00:08:42,841 --> 00:08:45,660
will return in a moment on Modern Marvels.

102
00:08:45,661 --> 00:08:54,480
The year was 1836, and the Erie Canal was under construction across upstate New York.

103
00:08:54,481 --> 00:08:58,160
A new digging tool was invented by some unknown Yankee.

104
00:08:58,840 --> 00:09:02,280
At the front, he placed a strip of iron as a cutting edge,

105
00:09:02,680 --> 00:09:07,240
and the device, pulled by draft animals, scraped up loose dirt and rocks.

106
00:09:07,880 --> 00:09:13,580
Hence the name drag scraper, the granddaddy of today's more sophisticated machines.

107
00:09:15,660 --> 00:09:25,360
Meanwhile, one of the transportation dreamers who spearheaded the canal, Robert Fulton, also paved the way in harnessing steam power to machines.

108
00:09:26,120 --> 00:09:29,180
His Claremont was the first to steam up the Hudson.

109
00:09:30,100 --> 00:09:33,560
Steam power was soon utilized in earth moving as well.

110
00:09:34,720 --> 00:09:39,660
The first earth moving machine was a machine called a steam shovel.

111
00:09:39,661 --> 00:09:43,880
William Otis was a young engineer, aged 20,

112
00:09:44,460 --> 00:09:49,720
and at that time he was thinking about better means of replacing hand labour

113
00:09:49,721 --> 00:09:51,580
and moving earth more efficiently.

114
00:09:52,480 --> 00:09:56,860
And he developed this new machine called the Yankee Geologist,

115
00:09:57,300 --> 00:10:00,600
and it's quite surprising to see pictures of that old machine

116
00:10:00,601 --> 00:10:05,280
and look at the modern machines and see how similar they are in overall appearance.

117
00:10:09,660 --> 00:10:15,340
The Ames Shovel Company had prospered, and Oliver Ames had turned the business over to his sons.

118
00:10:15,980 --> 00:10:20,960
Oaks Ames provisioned virtually the entire stock of shovels and tools for the Union Army,

119
00:10:20,961 --> 00:10:24,000
and became known as the King of Spades.

120
00:10:26,160 --> 00:10:30,200
Ames Shovels moved the earth and entrenchments from Gettysburg to Petersburg.

121
00:10:31,200 --> 00:10:39,180
In 1865, Abraham Lincoln asked Ames to take over a foundering project to build a transcontinental railroad.

122
00:10:39,660 --> 00:10:45,700
Oaks became president of the Union Pacific Railroad, and his brother Oliver became its

123
00:10:45,701 --> 00:10:47,876
chief point man in Congress.

124
00:10:47,877 --> 00:10:52,540
Steam locomotives would soon cross the entire continent, but

125
00:10:52,541 --> 00:10:56,980
the rail bed upon which they traveled would be built with earth moving technology that

126
00:10:56,981 --> 00:11:03,500
hadn't changed, using human sweat and Ames shovels.

127
00:11:03,501 --> 00:11:08,180
The Union Pacific Railroad opened the way for a whole new generation of farmers and

128
00:11:08,181 --> 00:11:13,680
earthmovers. Simulated by the railroad in the coming decades, steam power would also

129
00:11:13,681 --> 00:11:15,220
change the mining industry.

130
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:20,104
Doing this kind of digging started with John Henry versus the steam drill.

131
00:11:20,105 --> 00:11:20,880
Steam drilling

132
00:11:20,881 --> 00:11:25,160
equipment, steam excavating equipment, and then more and more mechanization and bigger

133
00:11:25,161 --> 00:11:26,120
and bigger machines.

134
00:11:26,980 --> 00:11:33,080
New earthmoving equipment harnessed the power of water, enabling Western miners to extract

135
00:11:33,081 --> 00:11:34,420
or at a quicker pace.

136
00:11:35,060 --> 00:11:38,620
By the 1880s, William Otis' steam shovel design

137
00:11:38,621 --> 00:11:41,900
was finally put into mass production by several companies.

138
00:11:42,900 --> 00:11:47,360
The most prominent of these were the Bucyrus Foundry and Manufacturing Company

139
00:11:47,361 --> 00:11:49,700
and the Marion Steam Shovel Company.

140
00:11:52,760 --> 00:11:55,740
The same technology was used in dredges,

141
00:11:55,980 --> 00:11:59,200
machines designed to scoop earth from the rivers and harbors

142
00:11:59,201 --> 00:12:00,920
of a growing industrial America.

143
00:12:01,900 --> 00:12:05,380
Meanwhile, early road contractors and railroad builders

144
00:12:05,381 --> 00:12:07,600
have long encountered earth-moving problems

145
00:12:07,601 --> 00:12:11,560
extending fills across swamps, bogs, and water holes.

146
00:12:12,460 --> 00:12:15,820
The Foley brothers hit upon the idea of pushing material

147
00:12:15,821 --> 00:12:19,520
ahead of a team of animals, instead of drawing it from behind.

148
00:12:20,620 --> 00:12:24,680
The horses or mules were hooked up to a steel plate, which they pushed.

149
00:12:25,440 --> 00:12:30,000
This was the early prototype of what was later to become the bulldozer.

150
00:12:30,920 --> 00:12:40,680
By the 1890s, the Austin Company of Chicago was leading the way in perfecting a dump wagon with a hinged bottom

151
00:12:40,681 --> 00:12:44,220
that could be used to quickly pour out the entire load.

152
00:12:45,160 --> 00:12:48,880
These early dump wagons soon evolved into dump trucks.

153
00:12:50,080 --> 00:12:54,640
Companies like Mack Trucks, founded in New York in 1902 and others,

154
00:12:55,020 --> 00:13:00,480
were quick to seize upon the possibility of combining the internal combustion gasoline engine

155
00:13:00,481 --> 00:13:06,040
with the wagon design to produce new vehicles for harvesting, hauling, and dumping.

156
00:13:08,380 --> 00:13:15,240
Back in 1867, Swedish chemist Alfred Nobel invented a new explosive process

157
00:13:15,241 --> 00:13:18,380
that was to indelibly transform earth moving.

158
00:13:20,180 --> 00:13:23,400
Dynamite, if you may or may not know, is nitroglycerin

159
00:13:23,401 --> 00:13:27,360
that has been packaged in with sawdust or something.

160
00:13:27,361 --> 00:13:31,420
to carry the nitroglycer, make it somewhat safer, dynamite,

161
00:13:32,060 --> 00:13:34,400
and made it possible to move mountains.

162
00:13:36,320 --> 00:13:38,540
By the dawn of the 20th century,

163
00:13:38,700 --> 00:13:42,540
Teddy Roosevelt spearheaded the construction of a canal across Panama,

164
00:13:43,160 --> 00:13:47,580
connecting the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans by literally moving mountains,

165
00:13:48,260 --> 00:13:51,340
where French contractors had failed 20 years before.

166
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:54,360
As an earth-moving challenge, the canal was made possible

167
00:13:54,361 --> 00:13:58,760
possible by the combination of the steam shovel and the railroad car.

168
00:14:01,480 --> 00:14:06,180
The Panama Canal was dug from 1904 to 1914.

169
00:14:07,220 --> 00:14:13,480
And at that time, the largest and most modern steam shovels were used to build it.

170
00:14:14,120 --> 00:14:16,940
The transportation of the material was rail transport.

171
00:14:17,180 --> 00:14:21,200
They laid temporary rail tracks, and rail cars were used with steam locomotives

172
00:14:21,201 --> 00:14:24,000
to haul the material away from the major cuttings.

173
00:14:24,001 --> 00:14:28,320
and there were as many as 102 steam shovels used on that project.

174
00:14:28,960 --> 00:14:32,760
These steam shovels were made primarily by two manufacturers,

175
00:14:33,100 --> 00:14:34,720
Becerus and Marion,

176
00:14:35,620 --> 00:14:40,220
and as we all know, the canal opened on time in 1914,

177
00:14:40,680 --> 00:14:43,420
and it's been a major seaway ever since.

178
00:14:47,560 --> 00:14:50,080
Just as digging on the canal got started,

179
00:14:50,340 --> 00:14:52,160
an epic-making breakthrough occurred,

180
00:14:52,161 --> 00:14:54,820
and it began in the farm fields of California.

181
00:14:56,660 --> 00:14:59,380
Benjamin Holt came west in 1883

182
00:14:59,381 --> 00:15:02,360
and set up shop in Stockton, California.

183
00:15:02,920 --> 00:15:04,840
His lifelong rival, Daniel Best,

184
00:15:04,900 --> 00:15:07,460
was a gold miner, hunter, and miller

185
00:15:07,461 --> 00:15:09,560
who tramped all over the west

186
00:15:09,561 --> 00:15:11,980
before settling in San Leandro.

187
00:15:15,120 --> 00:15:17,260
Unlike the flat plains of the Midwest,

188
00:15:17,500 --> 00:15:20,640
California's topography called for an improved combine

189
00:15:20,641 --> 00:15:23,600
that could bring more marginal land under cultivation.

190
00:15:24,600 --> 00:15:27,740
Holt came up with a new kind of side hill harvester.

191
00:15:28,640 --> 00:15:32,460
Best picked up the challenge, and soon the two were locked in a contest

192
00:15:32,461 --> 00:15:35,920
to see who could turn out the most gargantuan machine.

193
00:15:37,680 --> 00:15:40,200
As their rival machines went into action,

194
00:15:40,380 --> 00:15:43,380
they boosted wheat production by millions of bushels.

195
00:15:44,300 --> 00:15:47,300
The early tractors were, you know, just huge.

196
00:15:47,301 --> 00:15:52,040
You'll see pictures of ones with huge A-frames built and extended wheels,

197
00:15:53,180 --> 00:15:57,720
which of course meant that when you got those stuck too, it was just a bigger job to get them out.

198
00:15:58,360 --> 00:16:03,920
Bigger wheels were no solution in the spongy soil around Stockton in the rainy season.

199
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:11,240
Hold is credited with coming up with the idea that if you could have, like a set of planks

200
00:16:11,241 --> 00:16:15,580
that just laid themselves down in front of you, spread your weight out so you could stay on top of the bog,

201
00:16:15,581 --> 00:16:17,980
developing the first track layer system.

202
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:23,200
Holt and his men removed the wheels from steam traction engine number 77

203
00:16:23,201 --> 00:16:25,840
and installed a pair of new track units.

204
00:16:26,440 --> 00:16:29,720
The date was November 24, 1904.

205
00:16:30,600 --> 00:16:35,880
A temporary solution to a local problem would soon be multiplied millions of times.

206
00:16:37,340 --> 00:16:41,360
They put a set of tracks on a steam tractor,

207
00:16:41,361 --> 00:16:46,100
which was sold to a company down in Louisiana in the marsh down there.

208
00:16:46,460 --> 00:16:51,420
And the workers down there said, well, that looks like a caterpillar crawling along,

209
00:16:51,760 --> 00:16:53,440
and hence the name stuck.

210
00:16:54,220 --> 00:16:58,280
And so Holt's tractors became known as the Caterpillar System.

211
00:16:59,520 --> 00:17:04,200
Holt and Best, always rivals, now intensified their competition,

212
00:17:04,860 --> 00:17:08,240
each company perfecting its design to outsell the other.

213
00:17:08,241 --> 00:17:17,920
As the popularity of their machines caught on, one famous artist even suggested the crawler as a commuter's answer to muddy roads.

214
00:17:20,780 --> 00:17:26,000
Holden Best, talented inventor, entrepreneurs, not only mastered the art of production,

215
00:17:26,320 --> 00:17:31,580
but also used new techniques of salesmanship to sell their machines throughout the world.

216
00:17:31,581 --> 00:17:37,100
By 1910, Earth-moving machines were being exported everywhere

217
00:17:37,101 --> 00:17:41,020
from Russia to Indonesia and Argentina to Hungary

218
00:17:41,021 --> 00:17:43,840
launching a new era in Earth-moving

219
00:17:43,841 --> 00:17:48,720
The largest Caterpillar-driven transport

220
00:17:48,721 --> 00:17:52,180
is the 131-foot-long Marion 8

221
00:17:52,181 --> 00:17:56,120
used to carry spacecraft to their launch pads at Cape Canaveral

222
00:17:56,121 --> 00:17:59,900
Fully loaded, it can weigh up to 9,000 tons

223
00:17:59,901 --> 00:18:03,900
Construction machines will return in a moment on Modern Marvels.

224
00:18:06,840 --> 00:18:11,360
Benjamin Holt invented the Caterpillar tractor for peacetime use,

225
00:18:11,600 --> 00:18:14,500
but its development was propelled forward by war.

226
00:18:15,340 --> 00:18:18,020
Even before America entered World War I,

227
00:18:18,860 --> 00:18:23,480
General John Blackjack Pershing used Holt machines to supply his troops

228
00:18:23,481 --> 00:18:27,280
as he pursued Pancho Villa across the Mexican border.

229
00:18:27,281 --> 00:18:34,280
Crawler tractors replaced pack trains to haul supplies to US forces.

230
00:18:34,281 --> 00:18:39,246
August 1914, as war loomed over Europe, British General E.D.

231
00:18:39,247 --> 00:18:46,280
Swinton proposed building an armed land destroyer on a caterpillar type tractor base.

232
00:18:46,281 --> 00:18:55,280
The young first lord of the Admiralty, Winston Churchill, was quick to see the potential of this technology to create a formidable new weapon.

233
00:18:55,281 --> 00:19:02,860
Actually, the British Army took the concept of track layers seriously, and the Germans did not.

234
00:19:04,200 --> 00:19:13,060
The English sent Swinton over to visit the Holt facility, and they rapidly developed what was become known as the tank.

235
00:19:13,540 --> 00:19:15,460
The tank got its name in an interesting way.

236
00:19:15,540 --> 00:19:21,380
The English, of course, were trying to be secretive about their new weapon that was going to end the war in the trenches, essentially.

237
00:19:21,381 --> 00:19:26,520
and a passerby asked what, you know, all these machines that were laying out there

238
00:19:26,521 --> 00:19:29,720
and the comment was, is their water tanks were Mesopotamia.

239
00:19:31,120 --> 00:19:34,980
They weren't water tanks, of course, but the name endured.

240
00:19:36,360 --> 00:19:41,140
A special tank was presented by General Swinton to Holt in April 1918

241
00:19:41,141 --> 00:19:44,840
in appreciation of the great service rendered Great Britain

242
00:19:44,841 --> 00:19:47,740
by the Holt Manufacturing Company during the war.

243
00:19:47,741 --> 00:19:55,900
The dictates of combat also accelerated the development of everything from large cranes to giant steam shovels

244
00:19:55,901 --> 00:20:01,900
A young entrepreneur named Pauling teamed up with a German immigrant in Milwaukee named Harnischfager

245
00:20:01,901 --> 00:20:07,120
And together they founded a small company that would eventually become Giant, P&H

246
00:20:07,121 --> 00:20:14,060
The world's largest manufacturer of power shovels, huge excavators, and hydraulic drills

247
00:20:14,061 --> 00:20:17,540
War is a pretty good money-making proposition

248
00:20:17,541 --> 00:20:20,700
Certainly Holt and Best got rich off the idea

249
00:20:20,701 --> 00:20:23,480
But after the war, the markets got a little thin

250
00:20:23,481 --> 00:20:27,900
The pressures of overproduction, huge unsold inventories

251
00:20:27,901 --> 00:20:31,940
And canceled war contracts led Holt and Best to bury the hatchet

252
00:20:31,941 --> 00:20:33,400
And make peace as well

253
00:20:33,401 --> 00:20:36,320
Within a few years, the two companies merged

254
00:20:36,321 --> 00:20:39,040
But they kept the winning Caterpillar trademark

255
00:20:39,041 --> 00:20:42,380
Which soon created its own special fraternity

256
00:20:42,381 --> 00:20:50,940
If you're a good operator, you would be called a cat skinner, which I think kind of denotes the popularity of Caterpillar equipment.

257
00:20:51,580 --> 00:20:58,760
There were a lot of other manufacturers, but, you know, when you say Caterpillar, that's kind of generic for any crawler tractor anymore.

258
00:21:00,040 --> 00:21:05,740
After the war, improved mechanization signaled a new era of American road building.

259
00:21:05,741 --> 00:21:10,780
millions of federal and state dollars poured into new roads for the tin

260
00:21:10,781 --> 00:21:16,620
lizzie's being churned out by Henry Ford and other auto manufacturers in the

261
00:21:16,621 --> 00:21:22,640
1920s a new generation of improved bulldozers scrapers excavators and

262
00:21:22,641 --> 00:21:29,200
rollers change the look of the American countryside the first self-propelled road

263
00:21:29,201 --> 00:21:34,620
graders appear just prior to 1920 and his road construction boomed these new

264
00:21:34,621 --> 00:21:38,773
tools grew in weight, power, and popularity.

265
00:21:38,774 --> 00:21:43,020
This is a Caterpillar 60, and it's, you know,

266
00:21:43,080 --> 00:21:45,293
this built a lot of America.

267
00:21:45,294 --> 00:21:49,720
It's the last of the big gas burners up through the 20s.

268
00:21:49,721 --> 00:21:55,560
This one was built, I believe, 28 to 32, and it's, you know, it's very typical of earlier

269
00:21:55,561 --> 00:22:04,500
Caterpillars, big low RPM gas engine, simple, very rugged. One returning war

270
00:22:04,501 --> 00:22:09,059
veteran, Bob Letourneau came back to California broke and in debt.

271
00:22:09,060 --> 00:22:11,200
But he borrowed a whole tractor

272
00:22:11,201 --> 00:22:14,560
and began to design a new kind of earth scraper.

273
00:22:14,561 --> 00:22:17,780
With each refinement, Letourneau perfected his

274
00:22:17,781 --> 00:22:18,481
scraper.

275
00:22:18,482 --> 00:22:26,160
Before long, he was making big rigs like this 1929 Highboy, equipped with enormous steel

276
00:22:26,161 --> 00:22:29,233
wheels, being pulled by a Caterpillar tractor.

277
00:22:29,234 --> 00:22:32,640
Later, in a major breakthrough, Letourneau realized

278
00:22:32,641 --> 00:22:38,720
that big pneumatic rubber tires led to increased speed and greater hauling ability.

279
00:22:39,300 --> 00:22:45,260
So by the early 1930s, a new generation of scrapers and haulers were going to work.

280
00:22:45,520 --> 00:22:48,560
He didn't start small and then develop into larger machines.

281
00:22:48,561 --> 00:22:53,080
Very often the first machine of a type he would build would be the largest ever built.

282
00:22:54,220 --> 00:22:58,480
Letourneau's company was to prosper and grow over the next 40 years,

283
00:22:58,481 --> 00:23:03,840
fueled in part by the development of a revolutionary new kind of engine, the diesel.

284
00:23:05,200 --> 00:23:10,140
Early prototypes of this new motor had been around since 1893

285
00:23:10,141 --> 00:23:13,240
when a European engineer, Dr. Rudolf Diesel,

286
00:23:13,840 --> 00:23:17,200
confidently took out patents on his new engine design,

287
00:23:17,780 --> 00:23:21,120
predicting it would double the power resources of mankind.

288
00:23:21,880 --> 00:23:24,540
Diesel fuel was much cheaper than gasoline.

289
00:23:24,541 --> 00:23:32,300
but lightweight diesel engines were not available for use on earth-moving crawler tractors until the 1930s.

290
00:23:34,940 --> 00:23:40,880
Eventually, an improved method of road construction would lead by the end of the 1930s

291
00:23:40,881 --> 00:23:45,740
to the first of America's new superhighways, the Pennsylvania Turnpike.

292
00:23:46,580 --> 00:23:51,780
Contractors' fleets moved 26 million cubic yards of Pennsylvania earth.

293
00:23:51,781 --> 00:23:56,920
this new divided road was a glimpse of things to come.

294
00:24:00,320 --> 00:24:06,420
Other, even more gigantic earth-moving projects were part of Franklin Roosevelt's New Deal.

295
00:24:07,280 --> 00:24:10,980
In response to the grave unemployment of the early 1930s,

296
00:24:10,981 --> 00:24:15,860
the new president was determined to use government to put the country back to work.

297
00:24:16,620 --> 00:24:20,280
He and his administration embarked on a series of earth-moving projects

298
00:24:20,281 --> 00:24:22,880
that dwarfed everything that came before them.

299
00:24:24,120 --> 00:24:28,180
A great new dam took shape in Black Canyon on the Colorado River

300
00:24:28,181 --> 00:24:30,160
between California and Nevada.

301
00:24:31,120 --> 00:24:35,140
We stood behind Boulder Dam that was being constructed then

302
00:24:35,141 --> 00:24:39,200
just before they closed off the diversion tunnels.

303
00:24:40,100 --> 00:24:42,800
And so I was a young pup, a young kid, of course,

304
00:24:42,860 --> 00:24:46,040
and I got to see equipment that I couldn't believe.

305
00:24:46,480 --> 00:24:50,120
It was uncommon. A lot of it was made for that job,

306
00:24:50,121 --> 00:24:51,140
and that job only.

307
00:24:51,700 --> 00:24:53,460
And it was wonders of the world.

308
00:24:54,660 --> 00:25:00,040
It would probably equate with the Egyptians building the pyramids and so forth.

309
00:25:03,580 --> 00:25:11,880
Then you saw these huge, huge trucks that carried not only debris and you might say

310
00:25:11,881 --> 00:25:20,900
blasted rocker but they also carried huge pipes for the downside and the intakes of the power

311
00:25:20,901 --> 00:25:24,326
plant. It was truly amazing.

312
00:25:24,327 --> 00:25:32,400
Older style trucks were completely inadequate for a job like this.

313
00:25:32,401 --> 00:25:38,880
An entirely new generation of Mack trucks was designed with ribbed bodies curved inward at

314
00:25:38,881 --> 00:25:45,120
the bottom to withstand the impact of huge rocks.

315
00:25:45,121 --> 00:25:50,640
The company of Mac developed one of their famous trucks and these trucks were chain

316
00:25:50,641 --> 00:25:57,780
drive, very strongly built and they were really the first trucks designed solely

317
00:25:57,781 --> 00:26:04,880
for off-highway use as opposed to the lighter duty highway type trucks.

318
00:26:04,881 --> 00:26:11,680
This same mastery of earth-moving technology went into building the Grand Coulee Dam on the Columbia.

319
00:26:14,260 --> 00:26:19,640
And hundreds of new dams that dotted the Tennessee Valley and other parts of the country.

320
00:26:20,920 --> 00:26:28,440
Earth-movers traveling from job to job kept in touch, forming a loose network of friendships that remain strong today.

321
00:26:28,441 --> 00:26:37,580
No one in the 1930s knew how important the new dams would become a few years later.

322
00:26:37,581 --> 00:26:44,400
Without their electric power, America could not have transformed itself into Roosevelt's arsenal of democracy

323
00:26:44,401 --> 00:26:48,920
in the epic conflict that was looming just beyond the horizon.

324
00:26:50,900 --> 00:26:56,340
With the invention of steam power and then electrical and diesel-powered equipment,

325
00:26:56,341 --> 00:27:01,700
man's ability to move and transform the earth accelerated enormously.

326
00:27:02,420 --> 00:27:06,460
But this enhanced power also opened the door to new problems

327
00:27:06,461 --> 00:27:08,920
and unforeseen conflicts.

328
00:27:11,140 --> 00:27:15,860
The very earliest stripping shovel used in coal mining

329
00:27:15,861 --> 00:27:18,520
was developed by the Marion Steam Shovel Company.

330
00:27:19,240 --> 00:27:22,320
They came out almost every two or three years with a bigger machine.

331
00:27:22,321 --> 00:27:30,740
and by 1923 they had made the world's largest machine ever to move on land.

332
00:27:31,440 --> 00:27:36,440
That was quite phenomenal because it was built in the days before the era of welding.

333
00:27:37,420 --> 00:27:39,680
This huge machine was built using rivets.

334
00:27:40,280 --> 00:27:42,240
This was quite a major breakthrough.

335
00:27:43,800 --> 00:27:46,800
Now a drag line is a large piece of machinery

336
00:27:46,801 --> 00:27:50,360
but instead of having a dipper bucket in the front

337
00:27:50,361 --> 00:27:53,600
that you would dip up the rock and ore.

338
00:27:54,220 --> 00:27:57,140
You have a huge boom or a large boom

339
00:27:57,141 --> 00:28:00,080
with a shiv wheel on the end of the boom

340
00:28:00,081 --> 00:28:05,380
and cables that go over that boom and attach to a bucket,

341
00:28:05,800 --> 00:28:10,080
and the bucket is dragged into the machine until the bucket is full.

342
00:28:11,180 --> 00:28:14,420
When I was a child in a small rural community in Arkansas,

343
00:28:14,660 --> 00:28:18,480
just a few miles out of town, we had a strip mining operation.

344
00:28:18,481 --> 00:28:21,680
It was a subsidiary of a large national coal mining company.

345
00:28:22,120 --> 00:28:27,700
Back during the Depression, you saw that as a good job that paid $1 to $4 an hour,

346
00:28:28,380 --> 00:28:30,960
which was a big salary, big wage back then.

347
00:28:31,480 --> 00:28:35,180
And they saw it in one-dimensional, and that was pure economics.

348
00:28:35,380 --> 00:28:40,220
Nobody was thinking about the long-term effects of the damage this earth-moving equipment was doing.

349
00:28:40,980 --> 00:28:44,740
But when John L. Lewis, head of the United Mine Workers,

350
00:28:44,741 --> 00:28:50,280
stood up to the big mining companies and their enormous new machines, the implicit question

351
00:28:50,281 --> 00:28:54,760
was, what is the price in human blood for all of this progress?

352
00:28:54,880 --> 00:29:03,700
If we must grind up human flesh and bones in the industrial machine that we call modern

353
00:29:03,701 --> 00:29:12,200
America, we owe protection to those men first, and we owe the security for their families

354
00:29:12,201 --> 00:29:13,100
if they die.

355
00:29:13,101 --> 00:29:22,060
I say it, I voice it, I proclaim it, and I care not who in heaven or hell holds it.

356
00:29:27,600 --> 00:29:37,820
In the 1930s, economic depression and the struggle for global resources heated up as Germany, Italy, and Japan challenged the old rules of the game.

357
00:29:37,821 --> 00:29:48,400
Even before American entry into the Second World War, the earth-moving industry was gearing up for war production.

358
00:29:49,000 --> 00:29:55,300
Throughout 1940 and 1941, heavy equipment was already going overseas to Britain and Russia.

359
00:29:58,000 --> 00:30:04,080
American military designers asked Caterpillar to design a radically new air-cooled tank.

360
00:30:04,081 --> 00:30:08,500
A few months later, a test model was ready.

361
00:30:09,400 --> 00:30:12,440
Soon, M4 tanks were rolling off the assembly line.

362
00:30:13,120 --> 00:30:17,680
This new diesel tank could use heavy oil and low-octane fuel,

363
00:30:18,440 --> 00:30:20,220
an advantage in hilly terrain.

364
00:30:23,940 --> 00:30:29,540
December 7, 1941, the Japanese attacked Pearl Harbor by surprise.

365
00:30:34,080 --> 00:30:37,560
The entire earth-moving industry went to war.

366
00:30:38,680 --> 00:30:43,520
War production reached a fevered pitch in coal mining, logging and farming.

367
00:30:44,880 --> 00:30:52,920
Earth-movers embarked on crash programs, laying new pipelines, building war material plants and new highways.

368
00:30:56,520 --> 00:31:02,480
With Japanese forces on the move, Alaska was vulnerable to attack and invasion.

369
00:31:03,380 --> 00:31:08,380
The military saw a vital need for a highway to resupply Alaska through Canada.

370
00:31:09,260 --> 00:31:14,760
Work began on the Alcan Highway, the greatest construction project since the Panama Canal.

371
00:31:15,480 --> 00:31:19,780
It was dubbed the Road to Tokyo by the men who scrambled to build it.

372
00:31:20,840 --> 00:31:23,900
It was built in only two short summer seasons.

373
00:31:24,680 --> 00:31:29,820
It was a major effort, and they forged through swamps and woods and creeks

374
00:31:29,821 --> 00:31:32,880
and pushed this road through in just a phenomenal pace.

375
00:31:34,500 --> 00:31:38,960
By November 1942, the first Mack trucks roared north

376
00:31:38,961 --> 00:31:41,740
on the completed Alcan Highway to Fairbanks.

377
00:31:45,180 --> 00:31:49,680
In the Pacific campaign, the U.S. mounted a strategy of island hopping,

378
00:31:50,260 --> 00:31:53,880
landing men and machines, quickly establishing a beachhead,

379
00:31:54,140 --> 00:31:56,240
and moving on to the next battlefield.

380
00:31:56,241 --> 00:32:03,240
The Navy CBs built bases and landing strips virtually overnight.

381
00:32:03,241 --> 00:32:08,240
This is an international TD-14.

382
00:32:08,241 --> 00:32:12,240
These tractors were developed primarily for agricultural purposes.

383
00:32:12,241 --> 00:32:18,240
One of the interesting things, or certainly lucky things for us in World War II,

384
00:32:18,241 --> 00:32:21,240
is this heavy construction equipment is readily adaptable.

385
00:32:21,241 --> 00:32:27,240
Actually, there was very little change in equipment as it went on to the battlefield.

386
00:32:27,241 --> 00:32:32,240
The importance of earth-moving technology was dramatically apparent.

387
00:32:32,241 --> 00:32:39,240
When American fliers needed emergency airfields in China to land their bombers after attacking Japan,

388
00:32:39,241 --> 00:32:46,240
thousands of Chinese peasants used the same age-old method of shovels and baskets to carve runways.

389
00:32:46,241 --> 00:32:53,240
rise. Admiral Halsey summed it up when he said the four machines that won the war in

390
00:32:53,680 --> 00:33:00,680
the Pacific were the submarine, radar, the airplane and the tractor bulldozer. Earth

391
00:33:03,320 --> 00:33:07,299
moving equipment was just as important in combat in Europe.

392
00:33:07,300 --> 00:33:09,120
Following amphibious troops

393
00:33:09,121 --> 00:33:15,800
onto the bloody invasion beaches of Anzio and Normandy, huge bulldozers cleared mines

394
00:33:15,801 --> 00:33:19,960
and knocked out enemy pillboxes so battalions could move inland.

395
00:33:22,080 --> 00:33:27,440
The turning point came when the Russian army finally broke the back of the Nazi war machine.

396
00:33:28,200 --> 00:33:30,820
The greatest tank battle in history at Kirst.

397
00:33:31,320 --> 00:33:35,420
In 1942, 8,000 tanks fought to the death.

398
00:33:35,421 --> 00:33:42,600
In the aftermath, thousands of burned hulks, descendants of Benjamin Holt's California invention,

399
00:33:43,180 --> 00:33:45,080
littered the snowy Russian steppes.

400
00:33:45,800 --> 00:33:54,600
Back in America, companies like Bucyrus Erie and Marion

401
00:33:54,601 --> 00:33:59,040
manufactured earth-moving giants the likes of which had never been seen.

402
00:34:03,000 --> 00:34:07,080
By 1945, the giant dipper of one of these shovels

403
00:34:07,081 --> 00:34:09,780
could hold a Chevrolet truck or nine men.

404
00:34:10,860 --> 00:34:13,280
With the demands of wartime production,

405
00:34:13,281 --> 00:34:18,060
enormous new excavators, drag lines, and steam dredges were developed

406
00:34:18,061 --> 00:34:20,900
that dwarfed everything that had come before.

407
00:34:22,000 --> 00:34:25,060
And this was only a taste of things to come.

408
00:34:27,040 --> 00:34:33,360
The most powerful gantry crane is the Raco crane at the Grand Coulee Dam in Washington.

409
00:34:33,920 --> 00:34:42,580
It once lowered a 1,972-ton generator rotor with an accuracy of 1.32 inches.

410
00:34:42,581 --> 00:34:51,940
is construction machines will return in a moment on modern models the economic surge that followed

411
00:34:51,941 --> 00:34:57,220
the end of world war ii triggered another round of highway construction mining and building with

412
00:34:57,221 --> 00:35:06,660
ever larger and more efficient machines but along with progress came new concerns enormous strip

413
00:35:06,661 --> 00:35:12,500
mining machines were manufactured each year after the war these super strippers got bigger every

414
00:35:12,501 --> 00:35:14,891
year through the 1950s and 60s.

415
00:35:14,892 --> 00:35:19,520
This colossal stripping shovel, the Big Muskie, manufactured

416
00:35:19,521 --> 00:35:26,660
by Bucyrus Erie to dig coal in Ohio, was the biggest earth-moving machine ever made.

417
00:35:26,661 --> 00:35:34,840
It was nearly 22 stories high, and with its boom extended, was longer than one and a half

418
00:35:34,841 --> 00:35:35,880
football fields.

419
00:35:35,881 --> 00:35:44,840
weighing more than 150 Boeing 727 jet planes.

420
00:35:44,841 --> 00:35:49,700
It used more electrical power than a city of 100,000 people.

421
00:35:53,200 --> 00:35:55,580
Concern grew during this period, however,

422
00:35:55,780 --> 00:35:58,200
that strip mining and coal was devastating

423
00:35:58,201 --> 00:36:02,120
vast areas of West Virginia, Ohio, and other states.

424
00:36:02,121 --> 00:36:09,120
there were no reclamation laws and the land was physically not restored

425
00:36:09,121 --> 00:36:17,440
the problems with strip mining had been accelerating in the 50s and 60s in Appalachia

426
00:36:17,441 --> 00:36:25,820
and yet it wasn't until in 1972 there was a failure of a dam that coal miners had built up

427
00:36:25,821 --> 00:36:31,400
on a hillside in a place called Buffalo Creek in West Virginia and the material behind that dam all

428
00:36:31,401 --> 00:36:35,180
flooded down into the valley and drowned 125 people.

429
00:36:36,400 --> 00:36:41,780
Shocked by this tragedy, Congress passed legislation regulating coal mining

430
00:36:41,781 --> 00:36:45,300
and requiring restoration of the open coal pits.

431
00:36:46,100 --> 00:36:49,780
But the large coal companies did not concede without a fight.

432
00:36:51,140 --> 00:36:56,480
Nixon vetoed it because the coal industry said it would be the end of coal mining in America.

433
00:36:56,980 --> 00:36:59,940
Then Congress passed it again under President Ford,

434
00:36:59,941 --> 00:37:02,940
and Ford vetoed it again because he was listening to the coal industry.

435
00:37:03,460 --> 00:37:07,680
And then they passed it again in 1977 when President Carter signed it

436
00:37:07,681 --> 00:37:10,740
because he felt that this industry had to be brought under control.

437
00:37:11,900 --> 00:37:16,060
The legislation had a dramatic impact on the coal companies.

438
00:37:16,640 --> 00:37:23,120
Responding to the new 1977 law, the companies changed their techniques and priorities.

439
00:37:24,420 --> 00:37:28,700
Some major strip mining machines like the Big Muskie were closed down.

440
00:37:28,701 --> 00:37:36,320
Even more important, the companies developed cost-effective ways to remove the topsoil and the overburden,

441
00:37:36,980 --> 00:37:42,600
mine the coal seam, and then comply with regulations to restore the land.

442
00:37:44,260 --> 00:37:49,800
Soon, most of the industry moved out of Appalachia to western states like Wyoming,

443
00:37:50,360 --> 00:37:52,920
where the coal was more economical to mine.

444
00:37:54,920 --> 00:37:58,180
The biggest driveline working today is in the Powder River Basin.

445
00:37:58,700 --> 00:38:11,800
It's a machine called the Bucyrus Erie 2570 WS machine, and it has the name of Ursa Major, and it has a bucket capacity of 160 cubic yards.

446
00:38:14,240 --> 00:38:19,560
Today in the coal mining industry, computers are used to plan the contours of the land.

447
00:38:19,561 --> 00:38:30,520
Once the coal seam is mined, a new generation of earth-moving machines restores the environment to a form resembling its previous condition.

448
00:38:31,560 --> 00:38:34,560
But the new regulations cover only coal.

449
00:38:35,260 --> 00:38:38,960
Other sectors of the mining industry remain unsupervised.

450
00:38:38,961 --> 00:38:48,620
The only industries that I can identify for you which virtually get off scot-free are in the hard rock mining industry.

451
00:38:48,820 --> 00:38:54,520
Gold, silver, quartz, all of those kinds of things, palladium, platinum.

452
00:38:55,100 --> 00:38:57,160
Those are all considered hard rock minerals.

453
00:38:58,200 --> 00:39:01,380
And reclamation laws are not nearly as stringent as they ought to be,

454
00:39:01,381 --> 00:39:03,040
and certainly not as stringent as they ought to be,

455
00:39:03,180 --> 00:39:08,780
considering the fact that they take this stuff for nothing and the taxpayers, as they say, gets the shaft.

456
00:39:08,960 --> 00:39:16,240
Critics like Senator Bumpers and others say the old 1872 mining laws are inadequate

457
00:39:16,241 --> 00:39:21,460
because of the unprecedented scale of changes over the last few decades.

458
00:39:23,360 --> 00:39:32,385
In the last 15 years, the mining industry, in fact, has cut its manpower demands in the U.S.

459
00:39:32,386 --> 00:39:33,660
by about 80%.

460
00:39:33,661 --> 00:39:40,920
So we now only employ about 50,000 people to do the mining that used to require a quarter of a million people,

461
00:39:41,500 --> 00:39:44,520
and yet we're mining metals at a greater rate than ever before.

462
00:39:45,660 --> 00:39:52,980
Near Elko, Nevada, some of the biggest equipment on earth works the enormous Betsy Pitt mine.

463
00:39:53,760 --> 00:39:58,260
An immense and very rich $10 billion gold deposit.

464
00:39:58,261 --> 00:40:05,260
Huge trucks haul ore out of the pit at the rate of 240 tons a load.

465
00:40:05,261 --> 00:40:12,260
A decade ago, computer coordinated equipment like this didn't exist.

466
00:40:12,261 --> 00:40:25,260
Using massive pumps, earth moving engineers keep water from refilling the pit, improving the efficiency and speed of ore extraction.

467
00:40:28,260 --> 00:40:32,100
This same rapid development is seen in other parts of the country as well.

468
00:40:32,920 --> 00:40:38,880
Throughout central Florida, near Orlando, open-pit phosphate mines stretch far off to the horizon,

469
00:40:39,740 --> 00:40:43,980
where enormous modern hydraulic sluices pump out the mineral.

470
00:40:46,120 --> 00:40:52,340
The biggest man-made excavation on the planet is the Bingham Canyon Copper Mine in Utah.

471
00:40:53,300 --> 00:40:57,580
Copper mining has historically been one of the biggest of earth-moving operations.

472
00:40:58,260 --> 00:41:03,680
The ore volumes are very large, and they're spread out over wide areas.

473
00:41:06,440 --> 00:41:12,320
Wisconsin's 100-year-old P&H company recently completed a remarkable new behemoth.

474
00:41:13,140 --> 00:41:20,080
This earth-moving drag line, called the 90-20, has a crane longer than a football field,

475
00:41:20,860 --> 00:41:23,720
moves on state-of-the-art walking legs,

476
00:41:23,721 --> 00:41:29,240
and as its own built-in kitchen.

477
00:41:34,020 --> 00:41:38,480
One mining industry attorney I heard at a conference said,

478
00:41:38,481 --> 00:41:42,700
let's face it, gentlemen, we're not just impacting the environment at these sites,

479
00:41:42,840 --> 00:41:43,960
we're removing it.

480
00:41:44,720 --> 00:41:50,140
And that gives you a sense of the totality with which mining can come into a landscape

481
00:41:50,141 --> 00:41:55,600
and literally remove a mountaintop, turn what was a mountain into a huge open pit.

482
00:41:57,140 --> 00:42:01,120
Due to this unprecedented ability to move the earth,

483
00:42:01,480 --> 00:42:06,960
industry leaders and policy makers face new challenges and new responsibilities.

484
00:42:08,780 --> 00:42:15,980
One of the biggest problems from moving all of the material that these machines are pushing around at mines

485
00:42:15,981 --> 00:42:18,100
is what's called acid mine drainage.

486
00:42:18,101 --> 00:42:22,580
When you break it up so that it's no longer a big solid mountain,

487
00:42:23,180 --> 00:42:26,300
you release a lot of sulfur compounds in it.

488
00:42:27,040 --> 00:42:30,800
When it reacts with the air and with water to form sulfuric acid,

489
00:42:31,120 --> 00:42:32,820
then the acid flows downstream.

490
00:42:33,660 --> 00:42:36,420
It picks up other heavy metals and contaminants

491
00:42:36,421 --> 00:42:38,220
and carries them off of the mine site

492
00:42:38,221 --> 00:42:40,560
and into the rivers and streams around the country.

493
00:42:41,320 --> 00:42:45,400
The political cloud of the mining companies in this country is awesome.

494
00:42:45,401 --> 00:42:56,140
When you consider the magnitude of the problem that these people have created without paying the federal treasury a dime,

495
00:42:56,740 --> 00:43:06,500
and now the federal treasury, the taxpayers, are going to have to pay to clean up 557,000 abandoned mine sites.

496
00:43:07,560 --> 00:43:13,100
And while the problem is not as acute now as it used to be, the problem is still there.

497
00:43:13,101 --> 00:43:17,760
With the extraordinary development of these new machines,

498
00:43:18,160 --> 00:43:22,380
the need for responsible public policy seems more pressing than ever.

499
00:43:24,060 --> 00:43:27,760
The new earth-moving equipment gives us the opportunity

500
00:43:27,761 --> 00:43:31,820
to create bigger and bigger environmental messes,

501
00:43:32,260 --> 00:43:35,900
but it also gives us the opportunity to fix them.

502
00:43:36,640 --> 00:43:42,060
In the future, new technology and the ongoing public policy debate

503
00:43:42,061 --> 00:43:45,840
will lead to enormous changes in the earth-moving industry.

504
00:43:46,800 --> 00:43:49,820
In the meantime, the people we call earth-movers

505
00:43:49,821 --> 00:43:55,040
will continue to work and take pride in their amazing machines.

506
00:43:56,660 --> 00:43:59,640
Hard-drinking, hard-cussing, hard-working bunch.

507
00:44:00,400 --> 00:44:05,160
But I think all in all, a very moral bunch of people.

508
00:44:05,840 --> 00:44:07,360
You know, they're interested in building.

509
00:44:08,520 --> 00:44:10,200
They got that thousand-yard stare.

510
00:44:10,320 --> 00:44:11,600
You know, they're looking off into the future.

511
00:44:12,060 --> 00:44:16,820
and I think a lot of it is knowing what they're doing today.

512
00:44:16,940 --> 00:44:20,780
Somebody's going to look at 20, 30, 50, 100 years down the road

513
00:44:20,781 --> 00:44:26,700
and going to marvel at what they did with the technology they had at the time

514
00:44:26,701 --> 00:44:32,100
and how they could build something so durable to last over the generations.

515
00:44:42,060 --> 00:44:51,480
The largest earth-moving truck is the Teres T-Tau 3349, made by General Motors.

516
00:44:51,481 --> 00:44:59,740
It has a capacity of 350 tons, and its cylinder engine delivers 3,300 horsepower.

517
00:45:00,480 --> 00:45:04,480
Construction machines will return in a moment on Modern Marvels.

518
00:45:04,481 --> 00:45:12,320
Human earth moving has definitely accelerated in the past century or two

519
00:45:12,321 --> 00:45:18,960
and is now comparable to all of the other erosional processes.

520
00:45:19,460 --> 00:45:24,240
It all started so innocently roughly 400,000 years ago

521
00:45:24,241 --> 00:45:27,680
when Homo erectus started rolling big rocks around.

522
00:45:28,240 --> 00:45:33,040
They were moving boulders into position to hold posts

523
00:45:33,041 --> 00:45:34,720
running down the middle of the dwellings,

524
00:45:34,820 --> 00:45:38,000
and these posts must have required holes dug in the ground.

525
00:45:38,240 --> 00:45:40,580
And so this is some of the earliest evidence

526
00:45:40,581 --> 00:45:42,920
that we have of human earth moving.

527
00:45:43,460 --> 00:45:45,100
Soon we were digging holes,

528
00:45:45,620 --> 00:45:47,420
removing dirt in woven baskets,

529
00:45:47,660 --> 00:45:49,520
and digging out mud for bricks.

530
00:45:50,400 --> 00:45:52,120
We're still digging holes,

531
00:45:52,640 --> 00:45:54,440
but we're better at it now.

532
00:45:55,040 --> 00:45:56,020
A lot better.

533
00:45:57,520 --> 00:46:00,040
This is not a woven basket.

534
00:46:00,041 --> 00:46:09,760
It's a 320-ton Komatsu Hallpack 930E, currently the largest truck in the mining industry, though probably not for long.

535
00:46:09,761 --> 00:46:17,080
There's already some manufacturers talking about 360 tons, and there's probably going to be 400-plus tons trucks in the future.

536
00:46:17,320 --> 00:46:18,240
It wouldn't surprise me.

537
00:46:18,660 --> 00:46:21,380
Clearly, this is not your typical pickup.

538
00:46:21,920 --> 00:46:25,640
A man can walk underneath this vehicle without even bending over.

539
00:46:26,260 --> 00:46:29,680
The six tires have a radius of over 12 1⁄2 feet

540
00:46:29,681 --> 00:46:33,120
and cost over $30,000 each.

541
00:46:33,840 --> 00:46:37,140
The fuel tank carries 1,200 gallons of diesel.

542
00:46:37,880 --> 00:46:39,960
Most highways wouldn't last very long

543
00:46:39,961 --> 00:46:42,680
with the types of weights that we're talking about in these trucks.

544
00:46:43,560 --> 00:46:45,020
Neither would overpasses.

545
00:46:45,460 --> 00:46:48,140
This truck is 23 feet tall.

546
00:46:48,800 --> 00:46:51,760
It's basically just like driving a big old house

547
00:46:51,761 --> 00:46:53,780
with a cab sitting on top.

548
00:46:54,640 --> 00:47:00,760
Komatsu Mining Systems is one of about six manufacturers of these large off-road trucks.

549
00:47:01,520 --> 00:47:06,660
Their plant in Peoria, Illinois, began as the dream of a man named R.G. Letourneau.

550
00:47:07,960 --> 00:47:13,500
He manufactures scrapers, made his own welders, made wire rope, and basically founded this.

551
00:47:13,660 --> 00:47:16,340
So from that, we grew into off-highway trucks.

552
00:47:17,180 --> 00:47:19,960
Letourneau kept his engineers on their toes.

553
00:47:19,961 --> 00:47:25,260
He would go around the shop and some piece wouldn't work or wouldn't fit or whatever.

554
00:47:25,860 --> 00:47:33,481
He would either redesign it on the shop floor with chalk or he'd have an envelope in his pocket and he'd draw it on the back of the envelope and he'd drive the

555
00:47:33,482 --> 00:47:34,182
engineers.

556
00:47:34,183 --> 00:47:38,980
And that's just trying to keep up with him because those were the changes for the next machine.

557
00:47:40,040 --> 00:47:45,900
Letourneau's first haul pack truck was a 32-ton vehicle designed for use at mine sites.

558
00:47:45,901 --> 00:47:50,080
hauling giant boulders, tons of earth, or mounds of coal.

559
00:47:52,220 --> 00:47:56,600
He'd make the truck short so it would have a short turning circle, make it maneuverable.

560
00:47:57,360 --> 00:48:02,460
But the major thing he did was that the truck weighed less than it carried.

561
00:48:02,840 --> 00:48:05,740
And all the trucks prior to that, off-highway trucks prior to that,

562
00:48:06,100 --> 00:48:09,440
weighed a lot more than the payload that they carried.

563
00:48:09,620 --> 00:48:11,180
There's a lot of efficiencies gained with that.

564
00:48:11,181 --> 00:48:16,380
Trucks evolved from old dump wagons of the 19th century,

565
00:48:16,820 --> 00:48:19,680
designed to be able to lift and dump their haul of earth.

566
00:48:20,320 --> 00:48:23,040
With the advent of the internal combustion engine,

567
00:48:23,540 --> 00:48:26,840
companies like Mack improved on the horse-drawn wagons.

568
00:48:27,660 --> 00:48:30,180
Standard trucks that worked standard jobs,

569
00:48:30,640 --> 00:48:33,020
even giant projects like the Hoover Dam,

570
00:48:33,100 --> 00:48:35,920
were small enough that they could weigh more than their payload.

571
00:48:35,921 --> 00:48:42,180
But when the truly gigantic off-road trucks began in the late 1940s,

572
00:48:42,620 --> 00:48:48,940
their much larger payload capacity made Letourneau's lighter design much more practical.

573
00:48:50,500 --> 00:48:54,800
After Letourneau retired, the company passed through many hands,

574
00:48:55,060 --> 00:48:57,060
including ultimately Komatsu,

575
00:48:57,480 --> 00:49:03,600
which now builds a vehicle with ten times the hauling capacity of Letourneau's first haul pack.

576
00:49:05,920 --> 00:49:10,100
Things have changed since the days of blueprints on the back of envelopes.

577
00:49:10,760 --> 00:49:12,860
I supervise six other engineers,

578
00:49:12,861 --> 00:49:19,060
and we're responsible for the design of the hydraulic systems on the trucks,

579
00:49:19,220 --> 00:49:22,280
as well as the brake systems, brake application systems,

580
00:49:22,540 --> 00:49:26,160
and final drive design for the large electric trucks.

581
00:49:26,840 --> 00:49:30,440
The largest trucks are powered partly by diesel engines

582
00:49:30,441 --> 00:49:34,180
and partly by electricity generated by the vehicle itself.

583
00:49:34,960 --> 00:49:38,120
The diesel engine is connected to electrical generators,

584
00:49:38,780 --> 00:49:43,400
energy created when the trucks descend a hill is stored in batteries on the vehicle.

585
00:49:44,420 --> 00:49:49,480
That stored energy is then used to power the trucks when they ascend hills,

586
00:49:50,080 --> 00:49:52,360
sending power to the large wheel motors.

587
00:49:52,980 --> 00:49:54,400
The trucks have no transmission.

588
00:49:55,480 --> 00:49:59,580
Yes, it is more elaborate than your typical Ford or Chevy pickup.

589
00:49:59,900 --> 00:50:04,140
The Komatsu plant looks like a boneyard for dinosaurs.

590
00:50:04,180 --> 00:50:13,480
The assembly process for a 930 takes approximately 10 days from the time the frame is set in this building and the assembly process starts.

591
00:50:13,980 --> 00:50:17,820
Steel that makes up the body can be one and a half inches thick.

592
00:50:18,800 --> 00:50:21,340
The engine is 2,700 horsepower.

593
00:50:22,180 --> 00:50:25,680
The truck reaches a top speed of about 45 miles an hour.

594
00:50:26,420 --> 00:50:29,740
The volume of the bed is roughly 200 cubic yards.

595
00:50:29,741 --> 00:50:34,340
The vehicle is 27 feet wide and 50 feet long.

596
00:50:34,341 --> 00:50:40,740
Once the assembled truck is tested, it has to be disassembled and transported to the job site.

597
00:50:40,741 --> 00:50:47,740
Typically ships by rail requiring three rail cars to ship the wheels,

598
00:50:47,741 --> 00:50:53,740
the truck itself and its components, but that does not include the bed of the truck.

599
00:50:53,741 --> 00:50:56,740
Once on site, the trucks are in continuous use.

600
00:50:56,741 --> 00:50:57,441
use.

601
00:50:57,442 --> 00:51:04,120
Three shifts a day, 365 days a year for the life of the vehicle, which is estimated at 15

602
00:51:04,121 --> 00:51:08,500
years or more. That's around 100,000 hours of driving.

603
00:51:08,501 --> 00:51:11,420
Mines must really work the trucks to

604
00:51:11,421 --> 00:51:17,096
show a profit. A truck the size of the 930 costs over two million dollars.

605
00:51:17,097 --> 00:51:18,860
The name of the game in

606
00:51:18,861 --> 00:51:21,929
producing efficiently is asset utilization.

607
00:51:21,930 --> 00:51:25,640
When a customer such as a large mine buys a fleet of

608
00:51:25,641 --> 00:51:26,341
equipment.

609
00:51:26,342 --> 00:51:31,060
They're making a substantial capital investment, and they have to get every ounce of

610
00:51:31,061 --> 00:51:34,126
production out of that investment that they can get.

611
00:51:34,127 --> 00:51:36,720
These trucks are used in all kinds of mines,

612
00:51:36,860 --> 00:51:42,439
from coal to gold, but they are most commonly used in open-pit copper mines.

613
00:51:42,440 --> 00:51:43,540
Having eight or

614
00:51:43,541 --> 00:51:49,908
ten giant haul packs is cheaper and more efficient than having 40 or 50 smaller vehicles.

615
00:51:49,909 --> 00:51:50,609
In other

616
00:51:50,610 --> 00:51:57,620
where it's more economically efficient to move ore with larger and larger machines.

617
00:51:57,980 --> 00:52:01,040
And in an industry with a poor environmental reputation,

618
00:52:01,320 --> 00:52:06,300
these trucks produce less emissions per ton than smaller trucks and take fewer trips.

619
00:52:07,280 --> 00:52:12,200
The trucks are sized to match the shovels that do the actual digging on site,

620
00:52:12,201 --> 00:52:17,400
with optimum truck size being four passes or swings of the shovel per load.

621
00:52:17,401 --> 00:52:22,400
That's as much as 80 tons per shovelful.

622
00:52:22,401 --> 00:52:27,400
Big trucks like these have to be accommodated with special wide roads.

623
00:52:27,401 --> 00:52:31,400
It costs a lot of money in a mine to build a road for the trucks.

624
00:52:31,401 --> 00:52:35,400
They're changing all the time because they're making the mine wider all the time.

625
00:52:35,401 --> 00:52:40,400
So they have to keep the roads up and it's a big cost for them to do that.

626
00:52:40,401 --> 00:52:44,400
And if you're driving a regular-sized vehicle, you have to watch out.

627
00:52:44,401 --> 00:52:49,840
because up in the driver's seat of a 930, the blind spot is enormous.

628
00:52:52,120 --> 00:52:58,220
There are numerous incidents of pickup trucks being run over in mines by these trucks,

629
00:52:58,221 --> 00:53:04,460
and I will tell you, in a 320-ton truck, you can run over a pickup truck and not know it.

630
00:53:04,960 --> 00:53:08,980
For now, the 930 is at the top of the heap for big trucks,

631
00:53:09,460 --> 00:53:12,740
literally towering over every other truck in the industry.

632
00:53:14,400 --> 00:53:20,240
Getting more out of earth movers means getting more out of the earth.

633
00:53:20,740 --> 00:53:23,660
For Komatsu and the other big truck manufacturers,

634
00:53:23,920 --> 00:53:28,840
that means designing bigger and bigger vehicles to get more ore out of a mine.

635
00:53:29,560 --> 00:53:33,680
For other earth movers, it might not be precious metals they're looking for,

636
00:53:33,681 --> 00:53:37,180
but literally tons of soggy muck.

637
00:53:37,940 --> 00:53:39,780
Next, giant dredges.

638
00:53:39,781 --> 00:53:45,200
The Komatsu 930 E-Haul Pack Truck

639
00:53:45,201 --> 00:53:47,080
hauls in one truckload

640
00:53:47,081 --> 00:53:50,560
as much as 600 standard pickup trucks.

641
00:53:51,140 --> 00:53:53,400
Construction machines will return in a moment

642
00:53:53,401 --> 00:53:55,020
on Modern Marvels.

643
00:54:05,500 --> 00:54:09,500
The dredge Atlantic floats out in Charleston Harbor

644
00:54:09,501 --> 00:54:16,700
pulling up muck from the bottom of the channel and loading it onto a scow which will haul it out into the ocean and dump it.

645
00:54:17,980 --> 00:54:25,060
Dredges clean up shipping channels so that today's ships, heavily laden with goods, will not run aground.

646
00:54:27,080 --> 00:54:31,220
This dredge floats 40 feet above the earth it's moving.

647
00:54:31,800 --> 00:54:39,360
The Atlantic is one of the largest dredges on the eastern seaboard, capable of shoveling 32 cubic yards.

648
00:54:39,500 --> 00:54:43,040
the equivalent volume of an average-sized bedroom.

649
00:54:44,540 --> 00:54:46,540
This is a large job we're on now.

650
00:54:46,620 --> 00:54:48,800
It's about a $38 million job.

651
00:54:49,520 --> 00:54:51,820
The job in Charleston is two-pronged.

652
00:54:52,220 --> 00:54:54,940
The Atlantic is scooping out muck that is shoaled in

653
00:54:54,941 --> 00:54:58,040
or migrated from one part of the harbor into the channel.

654
00:54:58,880 --> 00:55:02,020
It is also digging the channel deeper than before

655
00:55:02,021 --> 00:55:05,140
in order to accommodate ships loaded to deeper depths.

656
00:55:05,141 --> 00:55:14,760
So we're actually doing about four feet of maintenance and another five or six feet of additional depth to increase the depth of the channel.

657
00:55:15,680 --> 00:55:26,127
The Atlantic, owned by Norfolk Dredging Company of Virginia, is a sophisticated, multimillion-dollar precision instrument using the latest global positioning systems

658
00:55:26,128 --> 00:55:27,520
and onboard computers.

659
00:55:28,220 --> 00:55:29,840
It wasn't always like this.

660
00:55:29,841 --> 00:55:38,640
Thousands of years ago, the Egyptians would swim to the bottom of the Nile with cloth bags to scoop up sludge.

661
00:55:39,400 --> 00:55:42,220
It was labor-intensive and required a lot of breath.

662
00:55:42,800 --> 00:55:47,220
But the Egyptians were able to remove silt runoff and muck that was filling in the river.

663
00:55:47,980 --> 00:55:51,500
As society began to depend more and more on shipping for trade,

664
00:55:51,501 --> 00:55:58,920
the need grew for deeper harbors to accommodate heavily laden ships packed with exotic goods from foreign lands.

665
00:55:59,780 --> 00:56:05,280
Maintaining these deep harbors and clearing up the silt and soil deposited by tides,

666
00:56:05,820 --> 00:56:10,000
agricultural runoff, and erosion became a daunting task.

667
00:56:10,940 --> 00:56:14,960
Mechanical dredging began around the 14th century in Europe

668
00:56:14,961 --> 00:56:18,940
and consisted of wooden planks chained together with buckets.

669
00:56:19,500 --> 00:56:22,940
The first steam-powered dredge appeared in 1804

670
00:56:23,640 --> 00:56:27,480
and was the product of Oliver Evans, an American inventor.

671
00:56:27,481 --> 00:56:35,220
steam engines would eventually operate buckets with four to six yard booms steel and the internal

672
00:56:35,221 --> 00:56:39,900
combustion engine would once again be the catalysts that would allow these earthmovers

673
00:56:39,901 --> 00:56:46,320
to grow into monsters the job itself hasn't changed much basically same thing just on a

674
00:56:46,321 --> 00:56:54,240
smaller scale uh with wooden holes or wooden booms excavating channels isn't the only job

675
00:56:54,241 --> 00:56:55,256
a dredge can do.

676
00:56:55,257 --> 00:56:59,640
In a strange earth-moving hybrid, some dredges are used in the mining

677
00:56:59,641 --> 00:57:03,202
industry to look for precious metals like gold.

678
00:57:03,203 --> 00:57:06,460
This abandoned dredge high in the mountains

679
00:57:06,461 --> 00:57:12,160
of Colorado was used to dig its own pond, where it would float, loaded up with the big

680
00:57:12,161 --> 00:57:14,263
rocks it had just excavated.

681
00:57:14,264 --> 00:57:18,620
The rocks would sift down into the dredge, sorted by size,

682
00:57:18,940 --> 00:57:22,620
for it was the smallest rocks that the miners were seeking.

683
00:57:22,621 --> 00:57:24,180
In the smallest rocks was

684
00:57:24,181 --> 00:57:29,780
gold, ten times heavier than water and more easily extracted than gold in the bigger rocks.

685
00:57:30,800 --> 00:57:35,760
Few of these old mining dredges survive, and fewer still continue to dredge for gold.

686
00:57:36,780 --> 00:57:42,180
The more traditional dredges built for widening and deepening channels certainly have grown up.

687
00:57:43,360 --> 00:57:45,351
The process hasn't changed very much.

688
00:57:45,352 --> 00:57:48,580
Years ago, dredging was a simple business for simple people,

689
00:57:48,581 --> 00:57:50,220
using simple tools.

690
00:57:51,220 --> 00:57:52,680
And some of it has changed,

691
00:57:53,100 --> 00:57:56,860
but mostly what has changed is your positioning equipment,

692
00:57:57,040 --> 00:57:58,720
how you maintain your position.

693
00:57:59,060 --> 00:58:04,640
And now it's all electronic, all DGPS, all satellite control.

694
00:58:05,280 --> 00:58:07,160
And that's not all that's changed.

695
00:58:07,560 --> 00:58:10,240
These dredging giants are a lot more efficient

696
00:58:10,241 --> 00:58:12,440
than the old wooden buckets ever were.

697
00:58:12,441 --> 00:58:16,100
With the size of the solid steel buckets the Atlantic uses,

698
00:58:16,101 --> 00:58:22,140
this dredge can fill up a scow that is able to hold 5,000 cubic yards of muck

699
00:58:22,141 --> 00:58:25,960
or 157 buckets full in no time.

700
00:58:27,100 --> 00:58:31,000
If you're digging in mud with a large bucket, big mud bucket,

701
00:58:31,100 --> 00:58:34,460
you can load the scow probably in two or three hours.

702
00:58:35,020 --> 00:58:37,080
Even the scows are state of the art.

703
00:58:37,740 --> 00:58:40,360
It's the Cadillac of all scows. It's real heavy.

704
00:58:40,580 --> 00:58:43,060
When the scows are full, it'll take this one away.

705
00:58:43,061 --> 00:58:46,340
It takes it out to the ocean and bottom dumps it.

706
00:58:46,720 --> 00:58:51,100
The scow actually splits right down the middle and dumps it out in the sink.

707
00:58:51,820 --> 00:58:54,880
High above the scow in an air-conditioned compartment,

708
00:58:55,440 --> 00:59:01,260
the operator manages the bucket on a diesel crane with a 48,000-gallon fuel tank.

709
00:59:03,200 --> 00:59:08,360
The operator monitors a GPS system that tells him exactly where to scoop.

710
00:59:08,361 --> 00:59:15,360
Locators on the dredge beam a signal to a satellite overhead, which then tracks the dredge.

711
00:59:15,361 --> 00:59:22,360
That shows me that we're on line, that I'm set up to where I need to dig, and so forth, all over the area.

712
00:59:22,361 --> 00:59:23,255
It's pretty easy.

713
00:59:23,256 --> 00:59:29,360
Once you got everything spread out pretty good and you know what's happening, you can go about anywhere you want to.

714
00:59:29,361 --> 00:59:35,360
Helping the operator keep the dredge in line is the dredge man.

715
00:59:35,361 --> 00:59:41,640
man. When the dredge is scooped up all it can in one section, the dredge slides down

716
00:59:41,641 --> 00:59:48,314
on a cable between anchored points. Unlike boats, dredges can't propel themselves.

717
00:59:48,315 --> 00:59:49,015
They

718
00:59:49,016 --> 00:59:54,520
are hauled into place by tugboats and maneuver around a site using anchors and chains.

719
00:59:55,080 --> 00:59:58,980
The whole Charleston Harbor here, we've got it on our computer disk and everything, and

720
00:59:58,981 --> 01:00:04,560
we can pop it up at any time and see exactly where we're going to be headed to and exactly

721
01:00:04,561 --> 01:00:05,860
how to get to it and set up.

722
01:00:06,460 --> 01:00:09,500
The entire job is sectioned off into stations,

723
01:00:09,900 --> 01:00:12,380
which can be completed in a 12-hour shift.

724
01:00:12,980 --> 01:00:15,480
The dredge scoops and then moves back and forth

725
01:00:15,481 --> 01:00:18,820
within each station until the area is cleaned out.

726
01:00:19,620 --> 01:00:21,640
With help from the computer system,

727
01:00:22,020 --> 01:00:25,800
this dredge can cover a lot of ground in a single shift.

728
01:00:26,360 --> 01:00:29,660
Sometimes 2, 3, 400 feet for a shift.

729
01:00:29,661 --> 01:00:32,200
It depends on how much material we're digging.

730
01:00:32,880 --> 01:00:38,860
The dredge uses different buckets, some with steel as thick as seven inches for different situations.

731
01:00:39,740 --> 01:00:44,220
If you were digging some hard clay or something like that, we'd have one bucket to go to,

732
01:00:44,221 --> 01:00:50,280
and then if we had some sand, we'd switch buckets to a bucket that would dig that.

733
01:00:50,860 --> 01:00:53,280
And then you've got buckets for just mud.

734
01:00:53,281 --> 01:00:57,780
The entire operation works with four- to six-person crews,

735
01:00:58,300 --> 01:01:02,300
shoveling 24 hours a day and maintaining the equipment.

736
01:01:02,301 --> 01:01:05,780
If it's broke, we'll fix it. If it's not broke, we'll paint it.

737
01:01:06,400 --> 01:01:11,220
Not surprisingly, the buckets occasionally bring up more than just mud and sand.

738
01:01:11,620 --> 01:01:14,860
If you can think of it, some dredge has dug it up.

739
01:01:15,360 --> 01:01:20,340
Everything from cannonballs, shark's teeth that are as big as your hand,

740
01:01:20,341 --> 01:01:25,780
all sorts of things, automobiles, airplanes, motorcycles.

741
01:01:26,240 --> 01:01:32,060
But their main function is to scoop up earth, and lots of it, before it fills in again.

742
01:01:32,740 --> 01:01:37,380
It will take Norfolk dredging about two years to dredge all of Charleston Harbor.

743
01:01:38,180 --> 01:01:41,560
When they finish, they'll likely have to go back where they started

744
01:01:41,561 --> 01:01:44,480
and repeat the process all over again.

745
01:01:44,820 --> 01:01:48,940
The rule that the shortest distance between two points is a straight line

746
01:01:48,941 --> 01:01:52,120
is one that has always propelled the tunneling industry.

747
01:01:52,940 --> 01:01:56,140
Instead of building a road around a mountain, you go through it.

748
01:01:56,800 --> 01:02:01,520
During the early Stone Age or Paleolithic period,

749
01:02:01,880 --> 01:02:07,180
people realized that they could dig using shoulder bones of oxen and things like this.

750
01:02:07,960 --> 01:02:11,480
Archaeological studies reveal that some early excavations

751
01:02:11,481 --> 01:02:14,240
were 30 feet deep and 30 feet in diameter.

752
01:02:14,740 --> 01:02:18,180
But you can only go so far with the shoulder of an ox.

753
01:02:18,940 --> 01:02:25,940
By the 19th century, explosives, drills, shovels and picks brought tunneling some sophistication.

754
01:02:25,941 --> 01:02:32,940
Tunneling and mining operations use these tools as their primary method of excavation for generations.

755
01:02:32,941 --> 01:02:45,476
In fact, the Comstock silver mines of Nevada and the Holland Tunnel are just two examples of the hundreds of jobs that use drilling and blasting to hollow out the

756
01:02:45,477 --> 01:02:46,177
earth.

757
01:02:46,178 --> 01:02:47,980
It's quicker than an ox shoulder.

758
01:02:48,580 --> 01:02:53,060
But it's not an exact science because dynamite isn't a surgical instrument.

759
01:02:53,180 --> 01:02:57,520
And it's labor-intensive because there's plenty of rock to remove from the side

760
01:02:57,521 --> 01:03:01,220
every time you blast or shovel or pick your way to the other side.

761
01:03:02,220 --> 01:03:03,820
But there's a better way.

762
01:03:04,420 --> 01:03:06,460
It's called a tunnel-boring machine.

763
01:03:06,940 --> 01:03:10,260
And according to Heron Connect, one of its manufacturers,

764
01:03:10,920 --> 01:03:13,820
it is the shortest distance between two points.

765
01:03:13,821 --> 01:03:16,480
It's basically like a glass cutter.

766
01:03:16,900 --> 01:03:19,320
It's pushed against the rock.

767
01:03:19,480 --> 01:03:22,140
As it's pushed against the rock, it forms an indentation.

768
01:03:22,640 --> 01:03:27,100
It's rolling, and then it creates fissures in the rock, which do the rock cutting.

769
01:03:27,700 --> 01:03:29,500
They started out in very small diameter.

770
01:03:29,660 --> 01:03:31,360
Early on, they were 12-inch diameter.

771
01:03:32,200 --> 01:03:35,340
Now you can drive a train through the holes they create.

772
01:03:36,020 --> 01:03:40,320
Some tunnel-boring machines even build the tunnel as they dig it.

773
01:03:40,321 --> 01:03:45,960
Nowadays, particularly in the last five or ten years, tunnel boring machines have developed

774
01:03:45,961 --> 01:03:51,200
what they call a single-pass lining, which is gasketed, bolted, and its segments, if

775
01:03:51,201 --> 01:03:56,660
you take a circular tunnel and they install segments, they could be in one ring, which

776
01:03:56,661 --> 01:04:01,740
could be composed of, say, seven to eight pieces, is installed directly in the machine

777
01:04:01,741 --> 01:04:02,800
as the machine is progressing.

778
01:04:03,480 --> 01:04:08,200
And as the machine progresses forward, then you actually have the permanent lining behind

779
01:04:08,201 --> 01:04:13,140
the machine and the tunnel is basically finished once the machine has finished the excavation.

780
01:04:13,960 --> 01:04:19,600
The tunnel bore works by gripping against the tunnel wall as the cutting head grinds away.

781
01:04:20,360 --> 01:04:24,680
The gripper slowly pulls the machine forward as the cutting head bores through the earth.

782
01:04:25,320 --> 01:04:28,760
It's like a giant can with one really sharp edge on it.

783
01:04:29,620 --> 01:04:34,500
It's much faster than the old drill and blast method and much more precise.

784
01:04:34,501 --> 01:04:41,040
It really depends on the ground conditions and the soft ground.

785
01:04:41,300 --> 01:04:44,480
We may only get around, say, 4 to 5 foot an hour.

786
01:04:45,080 --> 01:04:46,240
The ground is real hard.

787
01:04:46,500 --> 01:04:49,620
Just the hardness of the ground may keep us down around 3 foot an hour.

788
01:04:50,340 --> 01:04:53,980
For the ground's semi-soft, we can get up close to 12 foot an hour,

789
01:04:54,080 --> 01:04:59,200
if not exceeding that, depending on the diameter of the tunnel and the muck removing system.

790
01:04:59,620 --> 01:05:02,520
And how does that compare to the old drill and blast method?

791
01:05:02,521 --> 01:05:07,180
Well, I can compare it to the way we actually start the tunnels now.

792
01:05:07,280 --> 01:05:09,960
We have to actually do some drill and blast.

793
01:05:11,280 --> 01:05:15,000
And historically, we may be able to get six to eight foot a day.

794
01:05:15,500 --> 01:05:22,100
So we can usually do what they do in a day within one hour of actual operation.

795
01:05:22,700 --> 01:05:26,760
The precision of a tunnel bore can actually save the client money.

796
01:05:26,761 --> 01:05:33,080
One of the main advantages is that you get a cut diameter fixed all the way through.

797
01:05:33,840 --> 01:05:36,860
Your concrete cost, therefore, is almost unknown.

798
01:05:37,460 --> 01:05:43,300
Where if you drill and blast, you have a lot of overbreak, your cost of your concrete goes up considerable.

799
01:05:44,040 --> 01:05:50,640
That can amount to total savings on a job in the vicinity of 20 to 25 percent over drill and blast methods.

800
01:05:50,641 --> 01:05:58,640
The excavated material is removed in different ways depending on the hardness of the earth the bore tunnels through.

801
01:05:58,641 --> 01:06:04,640
The material actually, as it's excavated in a hard rock project, comes out through a conveyor.

802
01:06:04,641 --> 01:06:06,640
It's passed through the machine.

803
01:06:06,641 --> 01:06:12,640
Depending on the system behind the machine, it can come out through a conveyor.

804
01:06:12,641 --> 01:06:16,640
It can come out through what they call muck trains, which are basically containers

805
01:06:16,641 --> 01:06:19,640
just bringing the muck out like a normal train that you would see.

806
01:06:20,640 --> 01:06:23,000
In the soft ground machine, there's actually another way.

807
01:06:23,080 --> 01:06:26,620
We bring it out of the machine in almost a liquid form.

808
01:06:26,800 --> 01:06:28,120
We put a lot of water in there.

809
01:06:28,240 --> 01:06:30,700
The ground is cut up a lot finer,

810
01:06:31,360 --> 01:06:36,420
and then it goes out through pipes onto a conveyor belt or into a muck car.

811
01:06:36,680 --> 01:06:42,080
But we actually slurry it out from the cutting face itself.

812
01:06:42,820 --> 01:06:47,580
The tunnel boring machines are much better for building tunnels in urban environments

813
01:06:47,581 --> 01:06:51,500
where explosives and noise and traffic times can be unacceptable.

814
01:06:52,460 --> 01:06:55,000
And they're much better for building small bore tunnels,

815
01:06:55,120 --> 01:06:57,960
which are too small for people to actually climb into.

816
01:06:59,680 --> 01:07:03,200
Heron Connect manufactures all sizes of tunnel bores

817
01:07:03,201 --> 01:07:07,840
and currently builds the largest, a 47-foot diameter machine.

818
01:07:08,500 --> 01:07:11,100
The machines are manufactured at their plant in Germany.

819
01:07:11,101 --> 01:07:16,880
The factory does the complete fabrication, the complete machining, the assembly.

820
01:07:16,881 --> 01:07:20,080
It's assembled in the shop. It's fully tested.

821
01:07:20,400 --> 01:07:21,960
And then it's broken down into shipping.

822
01:07:22,700 --> 01:07:25,040
And then it's shipped to the particular job site.

823
01:07:25,480 --> 01:07:30,900
The assembly takes a little over a year, with the largest machines taking about 15 months.

824
01:07:31,620 --> 01:07:38,660
Those big machines can be 60 feet long, 400 feet including the earth removal systems behind them.

825
01:07:38,920 --> 01:07:42,360
And they can weigh up to 3,500 tons.

826
01:07:43,240 --> 01:07:49,060
Each machine Heron Connect builds is custom-made for a contractor working a specific job.

827
01:07:49,660 --> 01:07:54,600
When the job is finished, the contractor might disassemble and store the tunnel bore,

828
01:07:54,880 --> 01:07:57,940
or sell it, or rebuild it for another project.

829
01:07:58,760 --> 01:08:03,640
These sophisticated drilling machines might appear to be the pinnacle in tunneling technology.

830
01:08:04,020 --> 01:08:10,760
But Heron Connect and the other two or three tunnel boring manufacturers in the world are planning even better machines.

831
01:08:10,761 --> 01:08:17,260
I think the future of tunnel boring machines in particular is in automation.

832
01:08:17,920 --> 01:08:21,000
One day you'll see a tunnel boring machine, large diameter,

833
01:08:21,700 --> 01:08:25,920
excavating below a city with nobody actually physically in the tunnel.

834
01:08:27,000 --> 01:08:32,840
The excavation will be occurring remote control from an office above the tunnel.

835
01:08:33,500 --> 01:08:36,120
All the lining will be done automatically.

836
01:08:36,400 --> 01:08:39,660
All the equipment brought into the machine will be done automatically.

837
01:08:39,661 --> 01:08:42,500
and everything can be controlled from the surface.

838
01:08:43,460 --> 01:08:47,460
When tunnel bores push into the ground and pull Earth to the surface,

839
01:08:47,820 --> 01:08:49,000
it's a great achievement.

840
01:08:49,540 --> 01:08:53,700
When a plow turns the Earth upside down, it can be a disaster.

841
01:08:54,760 --> 01:08:57,640
Next, the plow that broke the planes.

842
01:09:00,400 --> 01:09:05,040
Some estimates place the amount of Earth moved by humans annually

843
01:09:05,041 --> 01:09:08,940
at six tons for every person on the planet.

844
01:09:09,620 --> 01:09:13,540
Construction machines will return in a moment on Modern Marvels.

845
01:09:16,140 --> 01:09:22,020
Giant trucks and dredges and tunnel bores move massive amounts of earth every day.

846
01:09:22,980 --> 01:09:26,780
Far more than an average farmer could hope to move in an entire year.

847
01:09:27,900 --> 01:09:33,460
Farmers move just a little bit of earth, just a few inches, with a humble and unassuming plow.

848
01:09:34,120 --> 01:09:40,620
Yet that simple revolutionary mechanism helped contribute to a series of catastrophes in the 1930s

849
01:09:40,621 --> 01:09:42,860
from which some are still recovering.

850
01:09:43,500 --> 01:09:44,240
The Dust Bowl.

851
01:09:45,020 --> 01:09:53,780
Drought, wind, over-farming, and the Great Depression conspired to turn what had been 97 million acres of lush farmlands into a desert.

852
01:09:53,781 --> 01:10:02,480
The fiercely independent and industrious pioneers who settled the prairie would have their hopes dashed as wind blew away the earth,

853
01:10:02,481 --> 01:10:06,120
blew away topsoil, even blew away the settlers,

854
01:10:06,660 --> 01:10:11,280
some of whom escaped just ahead of black rollers that blotted out the sun.

855
01:10:12,000 --> 01:10:15,880
You'd run for cover, and after a while you learned not to worry about the chickens and the cows,

856
01:10:16,260 --> 01:10:17,740
but to run for cover yourself.

857
01:10:18,200 --> 01:10:21,820
I interviewed people that said they would put in clotheslines

858
01:10:21,821 --> 01:10:26,600
or ropes between the barn and the house, or between the outhouse and the house,

859
01:10:26,601 --> 01:10:28,820
because you couldn't find yourself around.

860
01:10:28,940 --> 01:10:30,900
It was like being in a black blizzard.

861
01:10:30,901 --> 01:10:36,900
Throughout the history of agriculture, which began in Mesopotamia in the 9th millennium B.C.,

862
01:10:36,901 --> 01:10:42,680
a few key technological concepts have had a huge impact on civilization.

863
01:10:43,700 --> 01:10:46,780
One of the earliest was the invention of the plow.

864
01:10:47,880 --> 01:10:52,540
Early plows were made of wood, and as iron became available

865
01:10:52,541 --> 01:10:57,740
and was used for the actual cutting edges of the plows,

866
01:10:57,741 --> 01:11:01,160
that made plowing somewhat more efficient.

867
01:11:01,900 --> 01:11:05,980
Two technological breakthroughs would have a great impact on the Dust Bowl.

868
01:11:06,440 --> 01:11:10,900
One was steel, which was more readily available during the mid-19th century.

869
01:11:11,420 --> 01:11:14,480
Steel plow that could break that really tough prairie sod.

870
01:11:14,920 --> 01:11:17,120
And of course, once you broke it, it didn't heal.

871
01:11:17,121 --> 01:11:21,080
It was scarred forever, and the wind could get to it, and the elements could get to it.

872
01:11:21,460 --> 01:11:24,200
The other was the internal combustion engine.

873
01:11:25,100 --> 01:11:28,440
Tractors were starting to be used in the 1920s.

874
01:11:29,240 --> 01:11:32,040
Farmers could now plow 50 acres of land in a day,

875
01:11:32,820 --> 01:11:35,580
rather than three that they were doing with horses.

876
01:11:36,100 --> 01:11:40,280
The Great War created an enormous demand for wheat in 1914,

877
01:11:40,740 --> 01:11:42,660
and prices skyrocketed.

878
01:11:43,300 --> 01:11:45,920
Before the widespread use of motorized plows,

879
01:11:45,921 --> 01:11:49,420
just as the virgin prairie of southwestern Kansas was settled,

880
01:11:50,000 --> 01:11:53,340
farmers plowed under the grass and sowed the land with wheat.

881
01:11:54,200 --> 01:11:59,020
Acre by acre, the earth was turned upside down by the farmer and his plow,

882
01:11:59,580 --> 01:12:03,040
multiplied by thousands of farmers with thousands of plows

883
01:12:03,041 --> 01:12:06,720
on millions of acres of once virgin prairie.

884
01:12:08,220 --> 01:12:13,260
They called themselves sod busters, and they bragged about breaking the soil

885
01:12:13,261 --> 01:12:16,320
the way you would break a horse or break an animal, and that's what they did.

886
01:12:16,380 --> 01:12:17,080
They broke it.

887
01:12:17,081 --> 01:12:21,320
The demand and the price for wheat remained high throughout the 20s.

888
01:12:22,020 --> 01:12:25,000
Prosperity and good weather produced bumper crops.

889
01:12:25,500 --> 01:12:28,680
In fact, farmers saw a record wheat crop in 1930.

890
01:12:30,400 --> 01:12:34,320
Unfortunately, the stock market had crashed the previous October,

891
01:12:34,760 --> 01:12:38,700
and rural farmers were about to reap what Wall Street had sown.

892
01:12:39,060 --> 01:12:44,520
The bottom fell out, and wheat went from $1 a bushel to 25 cents.

893
01:12:45,020 --> 01:12:47,520
It now cost more to grow wheat than to sell it.

894
01:12:47,521 --> 01:12:51,820
So farmers stopped growing, and fields lay fallow.

895
01:12:52,460 --> 01:12:53,960
Then the rains dwindled.

896
01:12:54,660 --> 01:12:57,980
In a semi-arid environment, this was disaster.

897
01:12:58,680 --> 01:13:03,100
With acres of dry fields, prairie grassroot systems plowed under,

898
01:13:03,460 --> 01:13:05,000
and no rain on the horizon,

899
01:13:05,520 --> 01:13:09,260
the winds that whipped out of the Rockies blew up great storms of dust.

900
01:13:09,940 --> 01:13:12,420
Anyone who ever lived through it can tell you about it.

901
01:13:12,940 --> 01:13:16,300
It's a really frightening thing of having darkness at midday.

902
01:13:16,301 --> 01:13:22,680
They would wet bandanas or wet claws or towels and put them over their face like this

903
01:13:22,681 --> 01:13:25,560
to screen out the dust so you weren't breathing that stuff in.

904
01:13:25,720 --> 01:13:29,940
People died of what they called the dust bowl pneumonia, breathing in that dust.

905
01:13:30,320 --> 01:13:34,740
The drought continued for months, then years.

906
01:13:35,680 --> 01:13:41,860
Finally, families began leaving, abandoning their farms and heading for kin in other states,

907
01:13:41,861 --> 01:13:46,080
or just seeking out the hope of something better somewhere else.

908
01:13:46,300 --> 01:13:51,720
but most stayed and suffered because they knew of nowhere to run.

909
01:13:52,320 --> 01:13:53,980
Dust would blow in through the keyhole,

910
01:13:54,180 --> 01:13:58,860
so you wanted to get those wet towels and rags down to dust-proof your house.

911
01:13:58,940 --> 01:14:01,040
And, of course, it was never possible it would get in the chimney,

912
01:14:01,160 --> 01:14:03,000
it would get in through somewhere or other,

913
01:14:03,360 --> 01:14:06,520
and so whenever you came into your house, the damn stuff was all over,

914
01:14:06,521 --> 01:14:08,500
you'd rub a table and your hand would be dirty.

915
01:14:09,220 --> 01:14:13,540
Some counties in southwest Kansas had lost nearly half their population.

916
01:14:14,540 --> 01:14:18,540
Abandoned, uncultivated fields only fed the dusty winds.

917
01:14:19,420 --> 01:14:23,660
Earth that took thousands of years to create blew away in minutes.

918
01:14:24,200 --> 01:14:27,380
To have dust everywhere, know that you could never win that fight,

919
01:14:27,480 --> 01:14:31,240
that there was going to be dust all through your body, all through your clothing,

920
01:14:31,460 --> 01:14:35,600
through your food, that you couldn't escape it, must have been a nightmare.

921
01:14:36,200 --> 01:14:42,400
By 1934, over 650,000 tons of topsoil had blown away.

922
01:14:43,100 --> 01:14:47,900
After ten years of unrelenting drought, dust and depression, it ended.

923
01:14:48,520 --> 01:14:49,760
The rains came back.

924
01:14:50,820 --> 01:14:53,220
The rain made the soil less likely to blow.

925
01:14:53,760 --> 01:14:55,820
Grasses grew back in the dormant fields.

926
01:14:56,400 --> 01:15:00,000
Another world war soon raised the demand and the price for wheat.

927
01:15:00,660 --> 01:15:03,280
Prosperity returned to the Great Plains.

928
01:15:03,360 --> 01:15:07,800
But the farmers were determined to figure out why the Dust Bowl had occurred

929
01:15:07,801 --> 01:15:11,340
and what they could do to keep it from ever happening again.

930
01:15:12,180 --> 01:15:17,760
Beginning in the 1940s, the Wind Erosion Research Unit, located in Manhattan, Kansas,

931
01:15:18,260 --> 01:15:22,300
and part of the U.S. Department of Agriculture, has been doing just that.

932
01:15:23,020 --> 01:15:25,720
I think our unit exists because of the Dust Bowl.

933
01:15:26,580 --> 01:15:31,020
The Dust Bowl was instrumental in creating the Soil Conservation Service.

934
01:15:31,480 --> 01:15:36,120
It was dust settling in Washington from the Great Plains.

935
01:15:36,540 --> 01:15:40,500
The unit studied the causes of drought and the mechanics of dust storms.

936
01:15:40,501 --> 01:15:50,080
So our mission is to understand the processes of wind erosion, develop predictive tools and control practices,

937
01:15:50,600 --> 01:15:54,540
and then transfer this technology to the people who need and use it.

938
01:15:55,040 --> 01:15:59,460
The Wind Erosion Unit advised farmers on soil conservation techniques,

939
01:15:59,800 --> 01:16:05,660
from how to prepare the earth to which direction to plant crops, even how to till their fields.

940
01:16:05,661 --> 01:16:12,660
The wind erosion is more susceptible when the soil is dry.

941
01:16:12,661 --> 01:16:16,660
However, soil will be very dry even in a 90% humidity.

942
01:16:16,661 --> 01:16:21,660
The atmosphere is not nearly as important as the humidity of the soil.

943
01:16:21,661 --> 01:16:28,660
During the worst of the Dust Bowl, it was easy to blame farmers and their tractors for the environmental disaster.

944
01:16:28,661 --> 01:16:32,660
Many farmers sought ways to prevent a repeat of the dirty 30s.

945
01:16:32,661 --> 01:16:37,215
30s. They terraced their fields in order to conserve water.

946
01:16:37,216 --> 01:16:39,300
They rotated their crops to

947
01:16:39,301 --> 01:16:45,368
prevent depleting the soil. They developed irrigation systems to stave off drought.

948
01:16:45,369 --> 01:16:46,069
They

949
01:16:46,070 --> 01:16:48,795
planted trees as barriers against the wind.

950
01:16:48,796 --> 01:16:52,440
And they didn't use their tractors as intensely as

951
01:16:52,441 --> 01:16:57,277
before. Low and no tillage farming came into vogue.

952
01:16:57,278 --> 01:17:01,260
Now farmers would work with nature because

953
01:17:01,261 --> 01:17:04,980
Because ultimately nature is too big to break.

954
01:17:04,981 --> 01:17:07,660
The 1950s saw the return of drought.

955
01:17:07,661 --> 01:17:09,880
The skies again filled with dust.

956
01:17:09,881 --> 01:17:12,700
But this time the farmers were better prepared.

957
01:17:12,701 --> 01:17:18,720
New farming techniques saw them through the filthy 50s and the droughts that would follow.

958
01:17:18,721 --> 01:17:24,420
In the meantime, the government began buying up vacant land and returning it to grasslands.

959
01:17:24,421 --> 01:17:28,760
where they would buy back 160 acres at a time

960
01:17:28,761 --> 01:17:32,580
and let it go back to the prairie grass and the prairie dogs

961
01:17:32,581 --> 01:17:34,500
and to the antelope and the deer.

962
01:17:35,100 --> 01:17:40,020
The plow wasn't the only factor that contributed to the Dust Bowl of the 1930s,

963
01:17:40,021 --> 01:17:44,920
nor were farmers' culprits, solely responsible for turning the land to dust.

964
01:17:45,480 --> 01:17:49,380
Today we better understand natural cycles and economic ones.

965
01:17:49,920 --> 01:17:51,660
Farmers no longer bust the sod.

966
01:17:51,661 --> 01:17:56,920
They work it, they nurture it, and they support it as it helps support them.

967
01:17:57,440 --> 01:18:05,460
And when they do plow the land, they do so with the knowledge and experience learned from those dusty, windswept, and desolate days.

968
01:18:06,860 --> 01:18:12,180
Though plows disturb roughly one and a half trillion tons of earth annually,

969
01:18:12,560 --> 01:18:19,680
the majority of this soil is only moved a few inches with roughly one percent lost to erosion.

970
01:18:19,681 --> 01:18:24,680
Construction machines will return in a moment on Modern Marvels.

971
01:18:26,680 --> 01:18:31,680
Humans are moving dirt all over the planet at an unprecedented rate.

972
01:18:31,681 --> 01:18:36,680
Technology now allows us literally to alter the face of the earth.

973
01:18:37,680 --> 01:18:44,680
We are moving about 3.8 billion tons of earth a year in mining operations,

974
01:18:44,681 --> 01:18:50,680
operations, about 3 billion tons in road construction,

975
01:18:50,681 --> 01:18:59,680
and maybe 800 million tons in house construction.

976
01:18:59,681 --> 01:19:05,680
So that adds up to about 7.5 billion tons a year

977
01:19:05,681 --> 01:19:09,680
of Earth being moved in the United States.

978
01:19:09,681 --> 01:19:13,680
In fact, we are destroying Earth faster than it can be replaced.

979
01:19:13,681 --> 01:19:23,680
Yes, soil is made every day, created by seismic and environmental factors such as wind, gravity, rain, decay.

980
01:19:23,681 --> 01:19:26,680
And it is destroyed by the same factors.

981
01:19:26,681 --> 01:19:32,680
Rivers wash soil into the oceans. Wind blows dust into the atmosphere.

982
01:19:32,681 --> 01:19:42,680
800 years ago, according to my estimates, the rate of human earth moving doubled in about 400 years.

983
01:19:42,681 --> 01:19:48,280
Now it has tripled in the course of about 25 years.

984
01:19:48,281 --> 01:19:51,620
In fact, we may literally run out of Earth.

985
01:19:51,621 --> 01:19:57,300
As humans put greater burdens on the soil to support our ever-increasing population,

986
01:19:57,301 --> 01:20:00,800
we may have no place else to turn but up.

987
01:20:02,000 --> 01:20:03,800
Eventually life is going to leave the Earth,

988
01:20:04,080 --> 01:20:07,240
and I think the first step in leaving the Earth is going to be Mars.

989
01:20:07,840 --> 01:20:12,040
Now right now Mars is too cold and dry to support any life from Earth.

990
01:20:12,680 --> 01:20:17,240
Earth movers, or in this case, Mars movers, may change all that.

991
01:20:17,660 --> 01:20:21,460
The fourth planet from the sun is about one-half the size of Earth,

992
01:20:21,461 --> 01:20:25,260
has four distinct seasons, 24-and-a-half-hour days,

993
01:20:25,640 --> 01:20:29,800
and a mean temperature of minus 81 degrees Fahrenheit.

994
01:20:30,460 --> 01:20:31,980
But if we were to warm it up just a bit,

995
01:20:32,260 --> 01:20:35,840
then we could imagine seeding plants and ultimately taking animals to Mars.

996
01:20:36,220 --> 01:20:39,020
But there's a bigger problem on Mars than just the chill.

997
01:20:39,021 --> 01:20:43,240
The real problem for life on Mars is that there's no liquid water.

998
01:20:43,500 --> 01:20:46,160
Nowhere on the planet at any time is water a liquid.

999
01:20:46,480 --> 01:20:49,400
Now there's ice and there's vapor, but it's never liquid.

1000
01:20:49,840 --> 01:20:54,220
Carbon-based life forms like humans and plants need liquid to survive.

1001
01:20:55,220 --> 01:20:59,540
How do you turn ice and vapor into liquid water on Mars?

1002
01:21:00,180 --> 01:21:01,820
Enter Mars Movers.

1003
01:21:02,440 --> 01:21:05,720
To make Mars warmer, to make Mars a little bit wetter,

1004
01:21:05,721 --> 01:21:09,360
will require some kind of machines that we would send to Mars

1005
01:21:09,361 --> 01:21:13,700
that would generate the conditions which would ultimately allow biology.

1006
01:21:14,440 --> 01:21:17,100
You can think of those machines as sort of like gardening tools

1007
01:21:17,101 --> 01:21:20,020
that get the ground ready so that we can introduce life.

1008
01:21:20,560 --> 01:21:25,580
These machines, which might resemble this vehicle designed to explore asteroids,

1009
01:21:26,060 --> 01:21:27,980
would drive over the surface of the planet,

1010
01:21:28,120 --> 01:21:32,840
scooping up soil and processing greenhouse gases such as carbon dioxide,

1011
01:21:32,841 --> 01:21:35,980
the elements of which exist in the soil naturally,

1012
01:21:36,220 --> 01:21:39,880
so that they can be released into the thin Martian atmosphere.

1013
01:21:40,740 --> 01:21:42,920
So the dirt becomes a feedstock,

1014
01:21:43,060 --> 01:21:46,260
becomes material that these machines scoop up and process,

1015
01:21:46,620 --> 01:21:50,560
extracting the molecules needed to make these super greenhouse gases.

1016
01:21:51,200 --> 01:21:54,460
These Earth movers would help build a Martian atmosphere

1017
01:21:54,461 --> 01:21:56,100
that was thicker and warmer,

1018
01:21:56,600 --> 01:21:59,520
enough to melt frozen ice on Mars into water.

1019
01:21:59,920 --> 01:22:02,480
Eventually, the planet might be warm enough

1020
01:22:02,481 --> 01:22:03,840
to introduce plants.

1021
01:22:04,440 --> 01:22:08,360
Then, after the plants have transformed carbon dioxide into oxygen,

1022
01:22:09,040 --> 01:22:11,360
human colonization might come next.

1023
01:22:11,880 --> 01:22:15,740
But the first challenge would be getting the Earth movers onto Mars.

1024
01:22:16,280 --> 01:22:19,320
What I would propose we do is size them big enough

1025
01:22:19,321 --> 01:22:21,120
that they could be launched on a single rocket.

1026
01:22:21,580 --> 01:22:23,980
That's why they would probably be about the size of an automobile.

1027
01:22:24,520 --> 01:22:28,340
Land on Mars, open up their solar panels, and start doing their thing,

1028
01:22:28,480 --> 01:22:32,240
eating Mars dirt and releasing greenhouse gases, making Mars warmer.

1029
01:22:32,480 --> 01:22:37,720
operated remotely from Earth, they would be monitored and checked for reckless driving.

1030
01:22:37,721 --> 01:22:41,120
If we were driving a vehicle on Mars, remote controlling from Earth,

1031
01:22:41,500 --> 01:22:46,480
and we hit the brakes on Earth, it would take about 4 to 20 minutes

1032
01:22:46,481 --> 01:22:50,460
before the vehicle stopped on Mars, depending on where Mars is in its orbit.

1033
01:22:50,880 --> 01:22:54,400
Our Mars Earth movers would have some onboard intelligence

1034
01:22:54,401 --> 01:22:58,140
which could warn them against driving into boulders or off a cliff.

1035
01:22:58,760 --> 01:23:03,320
From Earth, two people might remote control a fleet of 50 or 100.

1036
01:23:04,180 --> 01:23:07,660
Someday, after Mars is warm and there's forests and people walking around,

1037
01:23:07,740 --> 01:23:11,220
there will be museums, and in the museum they might have the very first of these vehicles

1038
01:23:11,221 --> 01:23:13,660
that ever went to Mars to make the greenhouse gas and say,

1039
01:23:13,800 --> 01:23:17,080
this is what started it all, and maybe it'll still be working.

1040
01:23:17,540 --> 01:23:22,560
Some scientists speculate that day might come within the next 100 to 200 years.

1041
01:23:23,180 --> 01:23:24,280
Only one problem.

1042
01:23:25,220 --> 01:23:29,300
Well, we don't know exactly how we'd take Mars soil and make the compounds we need.

1043
01:23:29,700 --> 01:23:31,060
It's not theoretically impossible.

1044
01:23:31,300 --> 01:23:33,720
It's just someone's got to come up with a clever way to do the chemistry.

1045
01:23:34,100 --> 01:23:39,480
Maybe someday some Martian farmer will glance up over his shoulder at the big blue planet

1046
01:23:39,481 --> 01:23:45,320
and be thankful that we'd learn so much about Earth moving back when we lived only on the Earth.

1047
01:23:45,880 --> 01:23:49,480
Assuming Earth isn't blown apart by a collision with an asteroid.

1048
01:23:50,300 --> 01:23:55,040
Scientists speculate putting Earth movers to work to deflect these giant space neighbors.

1049
01:23:56,260 --> 01:24:02,280
We think there's anywhere from 1,000 to 2,000 objects about a mile in diameter or larger

1050
01:24:02,281 --> 01:24:04,380
that are within the Earth's neighborhood.

1051
01:24:04,900 --> 01:24:06,500
But it's a big neighborhood.

1052
01:24:07,100 --> 01:24:12,980
Scientists define near-Earth objects as those that get within roughly 30 million miles of Earth's orbit.

1053
01:24:13,680 --> 01:24:18,880
Nevertheless, something a mile across hitting the Earth would be cataclysmic.

1054
01:24:19,480 --> 01:24:27,780
The last large impact on the Earth probably took place 65 million years ago

1055
01:24:27,781 --> 01:24:33,100
when the dinosaurs were wiped out by an object that was six miles in diameter

1056
01:24:33,101 --> 01:24:35,700
and struck near the Yucatan Peninsula.

1057
01:24:36,060 --> 01:24:37,000
But don't worry.

1058
01:24:37,520 --> 01:24:40,540
The number of large near-Earth objects is small,

1059
01:24:40,720 --> 01:24:45,560
and scientists expect to hit only every 100,000 to 200,000 years.

1060
01:24:46,400 --> 01:24:50,900
So the object is to try and find the vast majority of these objects and keep an eye on them.

1061
01:24:51,380 --> 01:24:56,540
If they spy one headed in our direction, Earth movers will likely be ready to save the day.

1062
01:24:57,620 --> 01:25:03,460
Scientists have already theorized some strategies for dealing with these monsters from outer space,

1063
01:25:03,900 --> 01:25:08,660
including the most powerful Earth mover ever created, a hydrogen bomb.

1064
01:25:09,420 --> 01:25:14,160
The most direct and probably the most efficient way of doing it would be to set off a nuclear weapon,

1065
01:25:14,161 --> 01:25:19,900
not to blow it up, but simply to set it off near the surface of the asteroid

1066
01:25:19,901 --> 01:25:25,080
so the neutron flux from this neutron bomb would ablate the front side of the asteroid

1067
01:25:25,081 --> 01:25:29,140
and form a rocket-like thrusting of the material off the surface.

1068
01:25:29,660 --> 01:25:34,340
And if we can give the asteroid just a tiny thrust, maybe a centimeter per second or so,

1069
01:25:35,100 --> 01:25:40,380
over the 10 or 20 years that it would take for this asteroid to impact Earth,

1070
01:25:40,980 --> 01:25:43,740
we would have moved it enough so that it would miss Earth.

1071
01:25:44,160 --> 01:25:49,680
Or scientists could attach the engine of the space shuttle to the asteroid and drive it away.

1072
01:25:50,380 --> 01:25:56,000
Or erect a giant solar sail that catches sunlight and pushes the asteroid.

1073
01:25:56,500 --> 01:26:00,660
Or even attach a digging device that throws rocks into space,

1074
01:26:01,180 --> 01:26:04,840
creating a recoil that throws the asteroid into a different orbit.

1075
01:26:05,380 --> 01:26:08,720
But attaching things to asteroids can be slippery.

1076
01:26:09,300 --> 01:26:12,200
One of the problems we have is that there's so little gravity

1077
01:26:12,201 --> 01:26:15,820
that it's almost like driving your car on Earth on sheet ice.

1078
01:26:16,540 --> 01:26:20,000
Anchoring devices onto asteroids is one of the challenges

1079
01:26:20,001 --> 01:26:22,720
scientists feel confident they can solve

1080
01:26:22,721 --> 01:26:26,760
before any detected near-Earth object comes plummeting towards Earth.

1081
01:26:27,520 --> 01:26:32,060
In fact, we may choose to mine asteroids and comets for minerals and fuels

1082
01:26:32,061 --> 01:26:35,580
as we begin to colonize the inner solar system.

1083
01:26:35,920 --> 01:26:39,340
All sorts of new Earth movers are on the drawing board,

1084
01:26:39,341 --> 01:26:43,240
both for our own planet and for those yet to be explored.

1085
01:26:43,780 --> 01:26:47,220
What began as a simple effort to keep us sheltered and fed

1086
01:26:47,221 --> 01:26:50,040
has turned into an engineering legacy

1087
01:26:50,041 --> 01:26:53,680
that is a hallmark of the modern age, for good or ill.

1088
01:26:55,120 --> 01:26:57,680
We humans are the Earth movers,

1089
01:26:58,600 --> 01:27:03,620
designing bigger trucks and scoops and drills,

1090
01:27:04,020 --> 01:27:07,460
moving Earth and moving beyond Earth

1091
01:27:07,461 --> 01:27:11,760
into a frontier limited only by the scope of our imagination

1092
01:27:11,761 --> 01:27:14,980
and our ability to build what we see there.

